1
00:00:17,297 --> 00:00:19,597
(Handong in South Gyeongsang Province, the Alps of Korea)

2
00:00:21,367 --> 00:00:26,576
(Blessed with the energy of Mount Jiri...)

3
00:00:26,577 --> 00:00:30,217
(and the transparent Seomjin River flowing through the land)

4
00:00:31,147 --> 00:00:33,387
(Thanks to its pristine natural surroundings, )

5
00:00:34,117 --> 00:00:37,917
(fresh and healthy ingredients are abundant all year round.)

6
00:00:38,587 --> 00:00:41,457
(A place where history and tradition live on)

7
00:00:42,157 --> 00:00:46,127
(The siblings arrive in Hadong.)

8
00:00:46,767 --> 00:00:51,167
(Maegye Village, nestled at the foot of Mount Jiri)

9
00:00:52,337 --> 00:00:55,337
(Humming a tune, You Seok is the first to arrive.)

10
00:00:56,067 --> 00:00:59,337
(He walks in the rain along the path with light footsteps.)

11
00:01:00,047 --> 00:01:03,516
(Once again, he's the man with the flowers today.)

12
00:01:03,517 --> 00:01:04,547
Hello.

13
00:01:05,117 --> 00:01:06,887
Am I the first to arrive?

14
00:01:08,287 --> 00:01:10,057
Hello.

15
00:01:11,087 --> 00:01:14,487
(The man with the flowers is the first one to arrive.)

16
00:01:15,057 --> 00:01:18,427
(Take off your shoes before you come up)

17
00:01:22,397 --> 00:01:23,967
(Impressed)

18
00:01:25,497 --> 00:01:27,706
The mist is so beautiful,

19
00:01:27,707 --> 00:01:30,337
hanging between the mountains.

20
00:01:31,507 --> 00:01:32,867
It's especially atmospheric today.

21
00:01:33,177 --> 00:01:36,376
(The mist rising through the mountain valleys adds...)

22
00:01:36,377 --> 00:01:39,817
(extra beauty and charm to the scenery.)

23
00:01:41,017 --> 00:01:42,546
I can't believe there's a place like this.

24
00:01:42,547 --> 00:01:44,467
(Another person comes walking through the rain.)

25
00:01:47,757 --> 00:01:49,227
This place is so beautiful.

26
00:01:50,627 --> 00:01:51,696
(Smiling big)

27
00:01:51,697 --> 00:01:52,757
You Seok.

28
00:01:53,467 --> 00:01:54,467
Sun Young.

29
00:01:54,527 --> 00:01:57,097
Isn't this place so beautiful?

30
00:01:57,897 --> 00:02:01,567
Hello, I missed you so much.

31
00:02:02,707 --> 00:02:04,237
I haven't seen you in a long time.

32
00:02:04,337 --> 00:02:06,136
- We saw each other yesterday.
- Sun Young.

33
00:02:06,137 --> 00:02:07,206
Hello.

34
00:02:07,207 --> 00:02:08,806
(I'm talking to them, not you.)

35
00:02:08,807 --> 00:02:09,847
Have you been well?

36
00:02:09,977 --> 00:02:12,617
(Feeling bitter)

37
00:02:12,947 --> 00:02:15,817
- My goodness, You Seok.
- Come up here.

38
00:02:16,687 --> 00:02:18,387
- Did you sleep well?
- Yes.

39
00:02:18,487 --> 00:02:19,487
My dear You Seok.

40
00:02:21,487 --> 00:02:22,656
Have you been well...

41
00:02:22,657 --> 00:02:24,303
- during the last month?
- We saw each other yesterday.

42
00:02:24,327 --> 00:02:26,126
You can't say that here.

43
00:02:26,127 --> 00:02:29,167
Why not? Viewers, we always meet a day before.

44
00:02:29,867 --> 00:02:31,137
(This is not what I wanted.)

45
00:02:31,637 --> 00:02:33,166
(Meanwhile, the youngest comes to meet the older siblings.)

46
00:02:33,167 --> 00:02:34,237
What's this?

47
00:02:34,707 --> 00:02:37,937
(Looking around)

48
00:02:38,237 --> 00:02:39,923
I need to check everything that's growing in the fields.

49
00:02:39,947 --> 00:02:42,347
(The smart youngest scouts out potential ingredients first.)

50
00:02:42,677 --> 00:02:46,047
(Thinking of the siblings waiting for her, she runs through the rain.)

51
00:02:46,447 --> 00:02:47,447
Sun Young!

52
00:02:48,017 --> 00:02:49,987
- Welcome, Yoon Seo.
- You Seok.

53
00:02:52,057 --> 00:02:54,227
The pavilion looks so pretty.

54
00:02:54,657 --> 00:02:57,657
- Isn't this place beautiful?
- Isn't it amazing here?

55
00:02:57,957 --> 00:02:59,597
I love this place.

56
00:03:01,097 --> 00:03:03,436
I'm very touched. How did you even find a place like this?

57
00:03:03,437 --> 00:03:05,196
It looks like...

58
00:03:05,197 --> 00:03:07,536
- I love this place.
- Switzerland.

59
00:03:07,537 --> 00:03:09,836
- Jung Ah.
- Jung Ah.

60
00:03:09,837 --> 00:03:11,107
Hello.

61
00:03:13,077 --> 00:03:14,547
- It looks like a painting.
- Yes.

62
00:03:15,477 --> 00:03:16,546
It's like something out of a movie.

63
00:03:16,547 --> 00:03:18,828
(Like a scene in a movie, the eldest makes her entrance.)

64
00:03:19,317 --> 00:03:22,546
(Step by step)

65
00:03:22,547 --> 00:03:25,557
(She makes her way to meet her younger siblings.)

66
00:03:28,887 --> 00:03:29,957
What's the matter?

67
00:03:30,227 --> 00:03:31,227
Am I late?

68
00:03:31,727 --> 00:03:33,167
No, it's because you look beautiful.

69
00:03:33,527 --> 00:03:35,427
- Jung Ah.
- Hello.

70
00:03:36,027 --> 00:03:37,996
It's raining, but you still came looking so beautiful.

71
00:03:37,997 --> 00:03:39,773
- You look beautiful.
- We were admiring your look.

72
00:03:39,797 --> 00:03:41,206
- Did you look at the scenery?
- You brought flowers.

73
00:03:41,207 --> 00:03:43,837
Today's location is really beautiful again.

74
00:03:44,477 --> 00:03:46,637
(The puppy eyes are desperately seeking a compliment.)

75
00:03:47,907 --> 00:03:48,907
You Seok.

76
00:03:48,908 --> 00:03:49,977
(Seriously, You Seok)

77
00:03:50,817 --> 00:03:53,686
- This scenery makes you emotional.
- Isn't it amazing?

78
00:03:53,687 --> 00:03:56,856
People actually live here, right?

79
00:03:56,857 --> 00:03:59,757
- We'll be spending three days here.
- Right.

80
00:04:00,427 --> 00:04:01,487
It's so beautiful.

81
00:04:02,257 --> 00:04:03,327
This is great.

82
00:04:04,097 --> 00:04:05,456
Even the sound of the water is beautiful.

83
00:04:05,457 --> 00:04:08,767
(For the next three days, this beautiful village is our home.)

84
00:04:09,667 --> 00:04:12,637
(Now that everyone is here, shall we go to the house?)

85
00:04:12,967 --> 00:04:16,037
This has never happened. We never moved in on a rainy day.

86
00:04:16,237 --> 00:04:18,107
We never stayed at a house so nearby.

87
00:04:19,537 --> 00:04:20,606
(What will our new home in Hadong be like?)

88
00:04:20,607 --> 00:04:22,107
This place is really beautiful.

89
00:04:22,207 --> 00:04:25,017
Gosh, it says "Siblings' House."

90
00:04:25,617 --> 00:04:27,487
Really? Is this the house?

91
00:04:27,987 --> 00:04:28,987
- This is it.
- This is amazing.

92
00:04:28,988 --> 00:04:31,757
- This rock is so beautiful. No way.
- My goodness.

93
00:04:32,157 --> 00:04:34,487
Can you take a photo of me with the rock?

94
00:04:35,687 --> 00:04:37,056
I want to take a photo with this giant rock.

95
00:04:37,057 --> 00:04:39,356
(A commemorative rock photo to celebrate moving in)

96
00:04:39,357 --> 00:04:40,397
You look great.

97
00:04:40,797 --> 00:04:43,697
(If you move in on a rainy day, you'll live well in your new home.)

98
00:04:46,767 --> 00:04:47,767
My goodness.

99
00:04:48,267 --> 00:04:50,737
Jung Ah, look at this.

100
00:04:53,347 --> 00:04:55,847
- Gosh, this is incredible.
- Is it amazing?

101
00:04:56,207 --> 00:04:57,217
Look at this, Jung Ah.

102
00:04:57,717 --> 00:05:00,747
- This place is amazing.
- They found a great place.

103
00:05:00,847 --> 00:05:02,616
- My gosh.
- What's this?

104
00:05:02,617 --> 00:05:03,657
The house is really big.

105
00:05:04,117 --> 00:05:06,657
(After walking down a little trail)

106
00:05:07,127 --> 00:05:11,057
(Once you go through a tunnel of trees)

107
00:05:13,057 --> 00:05:16,227
(A beautiful tiled roof comes into view.)

108
00:05:17,167 --> 00:05:19,167
(Speechless)

109
00:05:19,897 --> 00:05:22,506
- It's like a nobleman's mansion.
- What is this place?

110
00:05:22,507 --> 00:05:24,553
(Overwhelmed by the grandeur, they exclaim endlessly.)

111
00:05:24,577 --> 00:05:26,307
Gosh, this is really...

112
00:05:26,807 --> 00:05:28,706
The veranda looks amazing.

113
00:05:28,707 --> 00:05:30,827
(A spacious wooden veranda overlooking the courtyard)

114
00:05:31,917 --> 00:05:35,887
(The four siblings' new home, the veranda house at Mount Jiri)

115
00:05:36,957 --> 00:05:38,016
(I love our house.)

116
00:05:38,017 --> 00:05:39,897
- This is a rich person's house.
- My goodness.

117
00:05:40,557 --> 00:05:42,027
- What is it?
- Is there someone?

118
00:05:42,857 --> 00:05:44,156
- What is it?
- Is there someone?

119
00:05:44,157 --> 00:05:45,157
(Laughing)

120
00:05:45,657 --> 00:05:49,067
(What's in the house?)

121
00:05:50,337 --> 00:05:51,567
- What is it?
- Oh, no.

122
00:05:53,537 --> 00:05:54,537
What's that?

123
00:05:54,538 --> 00:05:56,836
(Welcome...)

124
00:05:56,837 --> 00:05:59,406
(to our house)

125
00:05:59,407 --> 00:06:01,847
The photo is really suspicious.

126
00:06:02,377 --> 00:06:05,317
- We look like an eccentric family.
- My goodness.

127
00:06:05,677 --> 00:06:06,886
This is so embarrassing.

128
00:06:06,887 --> 00:06:09,447
Seriously.

129
00:06:10,317 --> 00:06:13,157
(It's funnier when you look it closely.)

130
00:06:13,487 --> 00:06:16,956
(The owners of this house look like they have quite a story to tell.)

131
00:06:16,957 --> 00:06:18,726
- Sun Young.
- Jung Ah, you look like...

132
00:06:18,727 --> 00:06:20,497
a woman married into this family.

133
00:06:20,727 --> 00:06:22,566
You look like a woman married into this family.

134
00:06:22,567 --> 00:06:24,596
- A daughter-in-law?
- I married into the wrong family.

135
00:06:24,597 --> 00:06:27,067
- You're the tough sister-in-law.
- Right.

136
00:06:27,437 --> 00:06:28,643
- And I look like...
- A disobedient daughter.

137
00:06:28,667 --> 00:06:31,037
And you're her husband.

138
00:06:31,137 --> 00:06:32,776
- You look like the type...
- Someone crazy?

139
00:06:32,777 --> 00:06:34,447
Who doesn't listen to anyone.

140
00:06:34,677 --> 00:06:36,817
Sun Young looks so hilarious.

141
00:06:41,417 --> 00:06:43,917
(Yoon Seo's favorite is Aunt Jennica.)

142
00:06:44,257 --> 00:06:46,986
- This is incredible.
- Sun Young, you look...

143
00:06:46,987 --> 00:06:48,057
(You're the best.)

144
00:06:48,927 --> 00:06:51,697
- This is so funny.
- Everyone has a strong character.

145
00:06:52,227 --> 00:06:54,967
If we went into town like that, everyone would avoid us.

146
00:06:57,767 --> 00:07:00,407
Men should sleep in this room. That room is bigger.

147
00:07:00,507 --> 00:07:01,566
What is it?

148
00:07:01,567 --> 00:07:02,647
There's something upstairs.

149
00:07:04,577 --> 00:07:06,007
(Eyes widening)

150
00:07:06,377 --> 00:07:07,446
My gosh.

151
00:07:07,447 --> 00:07:08,546
(A cozy space hidden under the roof)

152
00:07:08,547 --> 00:07:09,607
There's an attic.

153
00:07:10,077 --> 00:07:11,077
We have an attic upstairs.

154
00:07:11,078 --> 00:07:12,347
- Really?
- Yes.

155
00:07:13,887 --> 00:07:14,946
This is amazing.

156
00:07:14,947 --> 00:07:16,867
(The youngest ones fall in love with the attic.)

157
00:07:17,317 --> 00:07:19,986
Coming to this house makes my heart feel so at ease.

158
00:07:19,987 --> 00:07:21,263
(The siblings absolutely love their new place.)

159
00:07:21,287 --> 00:07:23,796
We can sit here and drink some tea.

160
00:07:23,797 --> 00:07:25,596
Is this a cushion you can sit on?

161
00:07:25,597 --> 00:07:28,097
(She gets interested in the bench at the veranda.)

162
00:07:32,137 --> 00:07:35,067
(Gently sitting on the bench on the other side)

163
00:07:35,667 --> 00:07:38,377
(Sun Young sits at the veranda for a long time.)

164
00:07:38,637 --> 00:07:41,707
I love this veranda. This is perfect.

165
00:07:42,677 --> 00:07:45,377
(She becomes one with the floor.)

166
00:07:47,247 --> 00:07:48,317
From above.

167
00:07:49,947 --> 00:07:52,557
Are you taking a photo of me? Why are you taking a photo of me?

168
00:07:53,557 --> 00:07:54,886
(Showing peace signs automatically)

169
00:07:54,887 --> 00:07:56,457
(Smiling)

170
00:07:56,997 --> 00:07:58,796
One, two, three.

171
00:07:58,797 --> 00:08:00,267
(Guys, look over here.)

172
00:08:01,527 --> 00:08:02,527
You look cute.

173
00:08:03,797 --> 00:08:07,367
(Saving their piece of new memory on the fridge)

174
00:08:08,907 --> 00:08:11,777
(Adding other shots of their move-in)

175
00:08:14,847 --> 00:08:15,907
We have to see...

176
00:08:16,677 --> 00:08:18,517
- what we will dig and harvest.
- Marsh clams.

177
00:08:19,817 --> 00:08:23,347
(You Seok is drawn to the local specialties marked on the map.)

178
00:08:23,857 --> 00:08:25,987
- I bet it'll be green tea.
- Green plums.

179
00:08:26,157 --> 00:08:28,787
There are eels and Chinese mitten crabs.

180
00:08:29,587 --> 00:08:30,596
Sweetfish?

181
00:08:30,597 --> 00:08:34,367
(There's a long list of things they could catch.)

182
00:08:35,067 --> 00:08:36,397
What if we're going eel fishing?

183
00:08:36,997 --> 00:08:39,267
(Their anticipation for the first labor increases.)

184
00:08:39,867 --> 00:08:40,937
The weather is nice.

185
00:08:41,467 --> 00:08:43,467
This village is like something out of a fairytale.

186
00:08:43,677 --> 00:08:44,806
Over there...

187
00:08:44,807 --> 00:08:46,976
There's even a book cafe.

188
00:08:46,977 --> 00:08:49,276
- Is there a book cafe?
- It says "book cafe."

189
00:08:49,277 --> 00:08:50,277
"Maegye?"

190
00:08:50,278 --> 00:08:51,776
- It's really nice.
- Gosh.

191
00:08:51,777 --> 00:08:53,577
A book cafe in the middle of the countryside?

192
00:08:53,647 --> 00:08:55,187
Seat together in Maegye.

193
00:08:55,917 --> 00:08:57,687
Seat together in Maegye.

194
00:08:58,357 --> 00:08:59,856
- You know what I'm saying?
- You're good.

195
00:08:59,857 --> 00:09:01,787
- Gosh, that's amazing.
- Yes, I know.

196
00:09:02,357 --> 00:09:04,996
There's a local restaurant too. Eat Together, Maegye.

197
00:09:04,997 --> 00:09:06,556
- There's a restaurant.
- Are we going to eat?

198
00:09:06,557 --> 00:09:07,567
Maybe we're eating here.

199
00:09:07,927 --> 00:09:08,966
There are people eating inside.

200
00:09:08,967 --> 00:09:10,266
(They see people eating inside the restaurant.)

201
00:09:10,267 --> 00:09:11,796
- There are people eating inside.
- Hello.

202
00:09:11,797 --> 00:09:13,437
My goodness.

203
00:09:14,137 --> 00:09:16,166
- Hello.
- Nice to meet you.

204
00:09:16,167 --> 00:09:17,207
Hello.

205
00:09:18,107 --> 00:09:19,136
(They go inside cautiously.)

206
00:09:19,137 --> 00:09:20,176
- Hello.
- Hello.

207
00:09:20,177 --> 00:09:22,477
(They go inside cautiously.)

208
00:09:23,047 --> 00:09:25,046
It must be lunchtime.

209
00:09:25,047 --> 00:09:29,087
(Villagers gathered at the restaurant for a meal.)

210
00:09:29,987 --> 00:09:31,457
The food looks delicious.

211
00:09:31,917 --> 00:09:32,987
It must be bibimbap.

212
00:09:33,557 --> 00:09:35,126
- It looks so good.
- It's very delicious.

213
00:09:35,127 --> 00:09:37,197
- It looks very good.
- My gosh.

214
00:09:37,527 --> 00:09:40,366
Do the villagers all gather here for lunch?

215
00:09:40,367 --> 00:09:43,197
- They get together twice a week.
- Twice a week?

216
00:09:45,197 --> 00:09:46,336
"Eat Together."

217
00:09:46,337 --> 00:09:48,307
(It's not an ordinary restaurant.)

218
00:09:49,237 --> 00:09:51,237
The women in the village...

219
00:09:51,907 --> 00:09:53,747
take turns cooking.

220
00:09:54,477 --> 00:09:56,006
We make dishes that we're good at.

221
00:09:56,007 --> 00:09:58,817
(A community meal made with everyone's signature dishes)

222
00:09:59,077 --> 00:10:00,686
Even if you live in the neighborhood,

223
00:10:00,687 --> 00:10:02,463
it's hard to see each other unless you make an effort to get together.

224
00:10:02,487 --> 00:10:04,216
Especially for the elderly,

225
00:10:04,217 --> 00:10:07,387
it gives us a chance to check in on them.

226
00:10:07,627 --> 00:10:08,786
That's one of the good things about it.

227
00:10:08,787 --> 00:10:10,133
(A special eating community at Maegye Village)

228
00:10:10,157 --> 00:10:11,397
First of all, the food is good.

229
00:10:11,927 --> 00:10:13,897
Sharing food together is wonderful.

230
00:10:15,327 --> 00:10:19,697
(Building warm bonds by sharing with each other)

231
00:10:19,897 --> 00:10:21,206
Do you get together every Wednesday?

232
00:10:21,207 --> 00:10:22,767
- Every Wednesday?
- Yes.

233
00:10:22,967 --> 00:10:23,976
- My gosh.
- Nice to meet you.

234
00:10:23,977 --> 00:10:25,207
See you again.

235
00:10:25,777 --> 00:10:27,146
- Nice to meet you.
- Enjoy your meal.

236
00:10:27,147 --> 00:10:28,207
Enjoy your food.

237
00:10:28,907 --> 00:10:31,847
It's so fascinating to be in the mountains like this.

238
00:10:32,477 --> 00:10:33,477
Look at the view.

239
00:10:33,717 --> 00:10:35,686
Where else would we drink tea with this view?

240
00:10:35,687 --> 00:10:37,286
Look at that view.

241
00:10:37,287 --> 00:10:39,287
- Look at that magnificent view.
- Seriously.

242
00:10:39,587 --> 00:10:42,827
(While the younger siblings are absorbed in the view, )

243
00:10:43,587 --> 00:10:44,626
(Jung Ah is thinking about something else.)

244
00:10:44,627 --> 00:10:47,197
The mist is really beautiful.

245
00:10:47,927 --> 00:10:51,436
By the way, we saw them eating at that place.

246
00:10:51,437 --> 00:10:52,437
Right.

247
00:10:52,438 --> 00:10:54,467
If we have enough time...

248
00:10:54,907 --> 00:10:57,377
We said we'd make a buffet sometime, remember?

249
00:10:57,637 --> 00:10:59,137
Maybe we could serve...

250
00:10:59,837 --> 00:11:01,607
- lunch there.
- Right.

251
00:11:02,147 --> 00:11:05,277
(Rewinding to before their first trip)

252
00:11:06,817 --> 00:11:08,246
(A meeting 3 months earlier)

253
00:11:08,247 --> 00:11:09,963
(Beneath the cherry blossoms in full bloom)

254
00:11:09,987 --> 00:11:11,586
At the local hair salon,

255
00:11:11,587 --> 00:11:13,317
- we could get our hair done.
- Our hair?

256
00:11:13,827 --> 00:11:15,186
We can do that.

257
00:11:15,187 --> 00:11:16,556
We can also go to the local market...

258
00:11:16,557 --> 00:11:17,696
and get matching outfits.

259
00:11:17,697 --> 00:11:18,897
At the village hall,

260
00:11:19,927 --> 00:11:22,567
- we could host a meal.
- Right.

261
00:11:23,227 --> 00:11:24,337
That sounds good.

262
00:11:24,667 --> 00:11:26,267
- That sounds great.
- That sounds good.

263
00:11:26,497 --> 00:11:28,267
Yes, that's a great idea.

264
00:11:28,437 --> 00:11:29,867
I'd love to.

265
00:11:29,937 --> 00:11:32,336
We could cook here in this kitchen.

266
00:11:32,337 --> 00:11:34,177
The kitchen is spacious and well-equipped.

267
00:11:34,947 --> 00:11:36,606
(Getting up suddenly)

268
00:11:36,607 --> 00:11:39,277
How many people usually have lunch here?

269
00:11:39,417 --> 00:11:41,716
Usually between 30 to 40...

270
00:11:41,717 --> 00:11:43,287
- I see.
- villagers come.

271
00:11:43,487 --> 00:11:45,287
A few from outside the village come too.

272
00:11:45,417 --> 00:11:46,456
- Forty people.
- Gosh.

273
00:11:46,457 --> 00:11:47,886
Forty people?

274
00:11:47,887 --> 00:11:49,057
Yes, 40 people.

275
00:11:49,587 --> 00:11:50,697
That won't be easy.

276
00:11:50,957 --> 00:11:52,597
We struggle to cook even for four people.

277
00:11:53,097 --> 00:11:54,526
We can do it.

278
00:11:54,527 --> 00:11:56,127
Of course, we can do it.

279
00:11:56,467 --> 00:11:58,397
We can make 40 servings. It's not like it's 400.

280
00:11:58,997 --> 00:12:01,006
(The planning meeting continues at another location.)

281
00:12:01,007 --> 00:12:02,007
(Thinking hard)

282
00:12:02,737 --> 00:12:04,836
Are we cooking just for the elderly?

283
00:12:04,837 --> 00:12:06,376
Or is the production staff eating too?

284
00:12:06,377 --> 00:12:07,476
Shall we make 100 servings?

285
00:12:07,477 --> 00:12:09,077
(What?)

286
00:12:09,277 --> 00:12:11,377
No, just cook for the elderly.

287
00:12:11,517 --> 00:12:12,823
- Hundred servings?
- Hundred servings?

288
00:12:12,847 --> 00:12:14,916
- We could grill fish...
- They were eating bulgogi earlier.

289
00:12:14,917 --> 00:12:16,386
- Right, bulgogi.
- Bulgogi and...

290
00:12:16,387 --> 00:12:18,016
- Bulgogi, - The food looked so good.

291
00:12:18,017 --> 00:12:19,987
- young radish kimchi.
- Sesame leaves.

292
00:12:20,417 --> 00:12:22,057
- We could make...
- And plums.

293
00:12:22,387 --> 00:12:24,173
- I'm sure we will find...
- braised pork ribs.

294
00:12:24,197 --> 00:12:25,197
- That sounds good.
- Right?

295
00:12:25,198 --> 00:12:27,396
- We could cook it in advance.
- Braised pork ribs.

296
00:12:27,397 --> 00:12:28,627
Exactly.

297
00:12:28,827 --> 00:12:30,536
There's something I want to make, but it'll be hard.

298
00:12:30,537 --> 00:12:32,137
- What is it?
- Dumpling soup.

299
00:12:32,997 --> 00:12:34,406
- Forget it.
- Right?

300
00:12:34,407 --> 00:12:35,906
Jung Ah, what about this?

301
00:12:35,907 --> 00:12:37,977
Something like a potato and cucumber salad.

302
00:12:38,177 --> 00:12:39,376
- A cucumber salad?
- A salad?

303
00:12:39,377 --> 00:12:40,677
- A salad.
- A potato salad.

304
00:12:41,547 --> 00:12:43,777
- A salad sounds good.
- Yes.

305
00:12:44,177 --> 00:12:45,347
We can also make jeon.

306
00:12:45,977 --> 00:12:47,017
Japchae.

307
00:12:47,847 --> 00:12:48,887
Chicken gomtang.

308
00:12:49,117 --> 00:12:50,817
- Soft tofu stew?
- Yukgaejang?

309
00:12:51,157 --> 00:12:53,087
And I'll make yakbab.

310
00:12:53,617 --> 00:12:55,487
Yakbab? That'd be amazing.

311
00:12:55,757 --> 00:12:57,726
If we have time today,

312
00:12:57,727 --> 00:12:59,597
why don't we make some kkakdugi?

313
00:13:00,967 --> 00:13:02,796
Do you know what the elders like?

314
00:13:02,797 --> 00:13:03,897
K Donuts.

315
00:13:04,337 --> 00:13:06,413
(A recommendation from someone who knows what the elders like)

316
00:13:06,437 --> 00:13:08,066
Aren't you saying what you want to eat?

317
00:13:08,067 --> 00:13:09,436
That's not true.

318
00:13:09,437 --> 00:13:10,637
They really like it.

319
00:13:10,737 --> 00:13:12,437
They really like that.

320
00:13:13,107 --> 00:13:15,306
I guarantee it. They would really like them.

321
00:13:15,307 --> 00:13:16,577
K Donuts and...

322
00:13:17,217 --> 00:13:18,416
M Pizza.

323
00:13:18,417 --> 00:13:19,816
"M Pizza?"

324
00:13:19,817 --> 00:13:21,246
(That's hilarious.)

325
00:13:21,247 --> 00:13:23,716
- I want N Pizza.
- N Pizza.

326
00:13:23,717 --> 00:13:26,187
We can contact No Pizza and M Pizza.

327
00:13:26,457 --> 00:13:28,457
- And frozen yogurt.
- Shall we order food?

328
00:13:28,957 --> 00:13:30,117
From the frozen yogurt place?

329
00:13:30,697 --> 00:13:31,756
I'd love some.

330
00:13:31,757 --> 00:13:33,156
(Speaking of which, she wants it.)

331
00:13:33,157 --> 00:13:34,596
- That's what...
- We aren't talking...

332
00:13:34,597 --> 00:13:37,496
- about what you want to eat.
- That's what I want to eat now.

333
00:13:37,497 --> 00:13:39,837
It looks like Sun Young is starving.

334
00:13:40,467 --> 00:13:42,107
- Seriously.
- Please give her some food.

335
00:13:42,407 --> 00:13:43,506
(They decide to take their time...)

336
00:13:43,507 --> 00:13:45,027
(to choose the menu for Eat Together.)

337
00:13:45,177 --> 00:13:46,736
Aren't you guys hungry?

338
00:13:46,737 --> 00:13:47,777
Yes.

339
00:13:48,247 --> 00:13:49,406
So we...

340
00:13:49,407 --> 00:13:50,407
(Surprised)

341
00:13:51,177 --> 00:13:52,617
(What is it?)

342
00:13:52,817 --> 00:13:54,546
- We prepared...
- I like chilled cucumber soup.

343
00:13:54,547 --> 00:13:56,647
- a nice, refreshing meal for you.
- Really?

344
00:13:57,017 --> 00:13:58,086
Why?

345
00:13:58,087 --> 00:13:59,487
(They're already suspicious.)

346
00:13:59,817 --> 00:14:00,886
Is it naengmyeon?

347
00:14:00,887 --> 00:14:02,587
I'd love some naengmyeon.

348
00:14:02,987 --> 00:14:04,196
That's what I want.

349
00:14:04,197 --> 00:14:05,296
(Just thinking about it gives them goosebumps.)

350
00:14:05,297 --> 00:14:06,297
(What could a refreshing lunch be?)

351
00:14:06,298 --> 00:14:07,466
A cucumber salad?

352
00:14:07,467 --> 00:14:08,767
I like chilled cucumber soup.

353
00:14:10,197 --> 00:14:12,117
We'll be generous and serve you lunch this time.

354
00:14:12,267 --> 00:14:15,466
(An applause of appreciation)

355
00:14:15,467 --> 00:14:17,137
There must be a catch.

356
00:14:17,167 --> 00:14:18,436
You'll be blessed.

357
00:14:18,437 --> 00:14:19,677
Jung Ah, you should trust them.

358
00:14:19,837 --> 00:14:21,777
Okay, we'll get going.

359
00:14:22,577 --> 00:14:25,247
- Be careful, Sun Young.
- Come this way.

360
00:14:25,647 --> 00:14:27,246
Where's your umbrella, You Seok?

361
00:14:27,247 --> 00:14:29,246
- I don't have one.
- Why do you get soaked on purpose?

362
00:14:29,247 --> 00:14:31,217
You look even better in the rain.

363
00:14:31,357 --> 00:14:32,717
Stay in the rain, You Seok.

364
00:14:33,157 --> 00:14:34,227
Wet hair.

365
00:14:34,927 --> 00:14:36,486
- I'm trying to look cool.
- That's tough.

366
00:14:36,487 --> 00:14:38,407
(The tough sibling who doesn't use the umbrella)

367
00:14:38,457 --> 00:14:39,897
You Seok, I'll drive.

368
00:14:40,197 --> 00:14:41,366
- I'll drive.
- It's okay.

369
00:14:41,367 --> 00:14:43,027
- I prefer to drive.
- It's okay.

370
00:14:43,427 --> 00:14:44,497
I'm fine.

371
00:14:44,997 --> 00:14:46,897
- I like driving.
- I'll drive on the way back.

372
00:14:48,337 --> 00:14:49,737
- Yoon Seo, are you driving?
- Yes.

373
00:14:49,907 --> 00:14:51,437
I'll drive you safely.

374
00:14:51,637 --> 00:14:53,677
- Drive slowly since it's raining.
- Okay.

375
00:14:55,247 --> 00:14:56,477
I appreciate it.

376
00:14:57,947 --> 00:15:00,417
(If we're ready, shall we go?)

377
00:15:00,847 --> 00:15:02,347
What will the refreshing meal be?

378
00:15:02,817 --> 00:15:04,017
I'm hungry.

379
00:15:04,987 --> 00:15:06,887
(Concentrating)

380
00:15:07,217 --> 00:15:08,587
Do you want a lollipop?

381
00:15:10,187 --> 00:15:11,867
Are you playing this song all of a sudden?

382
00:15:12,097 --> 00:15:13,327
Whose playlist is it?

383
00:15:14,827 --> 00:15:15,927
I put it on.

384
00:15:16,567 --> 00:15:17,697
Seriously, You Seok...

385
00:15:18,797 --> 00:15:20,336
(With the rain setting the scene, the siblings are ready to sing.)

386
00:15:20,337 --> 00:15:23,407
("My Love By My Side" by Kim Hyun Sik)

387
00:15:23,737 --> 00:15:27,346
(My love)

388
00:15:27,347 --> 00:15:30,777
(Please stay by my side)

389
00:15:31,417 --> 00:15:34,747
(They're feeling sentimental today.)

390
00:15:35,587 --> 00:15:38,717
(Is it because of the rain that won't stop?)

391
00:15:39,587 --> 00:15:43,326
(With a mellow tune, )

392
00:15:43,327 --> 00:15:46,957
(they drive through a tunnel of trees.)

393
00:15:49,127 --> 00:15:54,537
(The skies over Hadong are slowly beginning to clear.)

394
00:15:55,837 --> 00:15:57,507
(The mood is setting in.)

395
00:15:57,607 --> 00:15:59,007
You Seok, are you happy to be back?

396
00:15:59,437 --> 00:16:01,976
You said that you kept thinking about the four of us...

397
00:16:01,977 --> 00:16:04,447
- being in the car like this.
- I really like it.

398
00:16:04,917 --> 00:16:06,947
- It's very peaceful.
- Right.

399
00:16:07,147 --> 00:16:09,817
For the next three days, I want to forget about everything else.

400
00:16:10,787 --> 00:16:12,186
I'm so grateful...

401
00:16:12,187 --> 00:16:13,907
- that You Seok is happy here.
- Seriously.

402
00:16:14,487 --> 00:16:15,826
Isn't it the same for you, Yoon Seo?

403
00:16:15,827 --> 00:16:17,427
Yes, but...

404
00:16:17,997 --> 00:16:19,097
How shall I put it?

405
00:16:19,197 --> 00:16:20,896
I should also be good at expressing.

406
00:16:20,897 --> 00:16:22,097
(Staring)

407
00:16:22,297 --> 00:16:23,766
I can feel it all.

408
00:16:23,767 --> 00:16:26,566
I can feel it from 45°...

409
00:16:26,567 --> 00:16:28,907
in front of your right eye.

410
00:16:29,667 --> 00:16:30,806
Especially when you move the third...

411
00:16:30,807 --> 00:16:32,907
- But I...
- eyelash on your right eye,

412
00:16:33,507 --> 00:16:34,576
I can feel it.

413
00:16:34,577 --> 00:16:36,306
Whatever I say is true.

414
00:16:36,307 --> 00:16:38,253
(Even if she's not good with words, she feels the same way.)

415
00:16:38,277 --> 00:16:40,847
"A good place to raise kid, Gwangyang?"

416
00:16:41,287 --> 00:16:42,547
Are we in Gwangyang?

417
00:16:43,387 --> 00:16:44,416
- What?
- We're in Gwangyang.

418
00:16:44,417 --> 00:16:46,017
Did we come all the way to Gwangyang?

419
00:16:47,157 --> 00:16:48,656
No, we're still in Hadong.

420
00:16:48,657 --> 00:16:49,756
Between Hadong and Gwangyang...

421
00:16:49,757 --> 00:16:50,926
I guess...

422
00:16:50,927 --> 00:16:52,356
Gwangyang is just across that bridge.

423
00:16:52,357 --> 00:16:53,557
(Eureka)

424
00:16:54,597 --> 00:16:56,226
(The Seomjin River flows towards Namhae, )

425
00:16:56,227 --> 00:16:58,367
(forming the border between Hadong and Gwangyang.)

426
00:16:59,737 --> 00:17:00,737
(Cross just one bridge, )

427
00:17:00,738 --> 00:17:02,436
(and you can travel between Hadong and Gwangyang.)

428
00:17:02,437 --> 00:17:04,106
There's Seomjin River...

429
00:17:04,107 --> 00:17:05,907
between Gwangyang and Hadong.

430
00:17:06,377 --> 00:17:07,806
(Hadong, Gwangyang, Gurye)

431
00:17:07,807 --> 00:17:09,907
(They're very close, aren't they?)

432
00:17:10,077 --> 00:17:11,077
Is that right?

433
00:17:11,078 --> 00:17:12,976
(I see.)

434
00:17:12,977 --> 00:17:14,886
(Stretching across...)

435
00:17:14,887 --> 00:17:16,447
(Jeolla and Gyeongsang Provinces)

436
00:17:16,717 --> 00:17:18,486
("Hwagae Market" is...)

437
00:17:18,487 --> 00:17:20,416
(irresistible.)

438
00:17:20,417 --> 00:17:21,886
(The Seomjin River is...)

439
00:17:21,887 --> 00:17:24,027
(next to Hadong and Gwangyang.)

440
00:17:24,457 --> 00:17:25,857
Shall we listen to "Hwagae Market?"

441
00:17:27,057 --> 00:17:28,926
("Hwagae Market" by Jo Young Nam)

442
00:17:28,927 --> 00:17:30,097
We have to listen to this.

443
00:17:31,967 --> 00:17:33,596
(Excited)

444
00:17:33,597 --> 00:17:36,466
On "Korea Sings," Jo Young Nam sang...

445
00:17:36,467 --> 00:17:38,007
that song very often.

446
00:17:38,537 --> 00:17:40,376
(It has everything)

447
00:17:40,377 --> 00:17:42,277
(That you need)

448
00:17:42,577 --> 00:17:44,576
(It has everything)

449
00:17:44,577 --> 00:17:47,987
(Hwagae Market)

450
00:17:49,047 --> 00:17:52,357
(Singing along at the top of their lungs)

451
00:17:52,587 --> 00:17:53,787
I can't wait to find out.

452
00:17:54,157 --> 00:17:55,587
I think we're here.

453
00:17:56,757 --> 00:17:57,997
Where are we?

454
00:17:58,527 --> 00:18:00,127
Aren't we going to Seomjin River?

455
00:18:00,627 --> 00:18:02,426
(Seomjin River?)

456
00:18:02,427 --> 00:18:03,667
- Where are we?
- What's this?

457
00:18:04,767 --> 00:18:06,936
We're at Seomjin River.

458
00:18:06,937 --> 00:18:08,136
(They suddenly find themselves by the Seomjin River.)

459
00:18:08,137 --> 00:18:09,167
My goodness.

460
00:18:09,337 --> 00:18:10,366
- What?
- What?

461
00:18:10,367 --> 00:18:11,376
(Everyone is flustered.)

462
00:18:11,377 --> 00:18:12,607
Where are we eating?

463
00:18:13,837 --> 00:18:17,577
(It doesn't look like there's a refreshing restaurant.)

464
00:18:17,977 --> 00:18:19,847
Didn't they say that we'll eat first?

465
00:18:20,547 --> 00:18:23,487
(A flashback of memories from a moment ago)

466
00:18:24,117 --> 00:18:25,556
Where are we eating?

467
00:18:25,557 --> 00:18:26,916
(Are we...)

468
00:18:26,917 --> 00:18:28,157
(actually going to eat?)

469
00:18:29,827 --> 00:18:30,957
What about food?

470
00:18:32,227 --> 00:18:33,926
(They somehow ended up at the Seomjin River.)

471
00:18:33,927 --> 00:18:35,127
They said that they'd feed us.

472
00:18:35,567 --> 00:18:36,727
Food.

473
00:18:37,167 --> 00:18:38,237
There's something.

474
00:18:38,837 --> 00:18:39,837
(Exclaiming)

475
00:18:41,337 --> 00:18:43,637
(What is it?)

476
00:18:45,037 --> 00:18:46,177
Gosh, what's this?

477
00:18:49,047 --> 00:18:51,976
(The Seomjin River stretching out endlessly)

478
00:18:51,977 --> 00:18:53,377
Where's our food?

479
00:18:54,117 --> 00:18:56,037
You said that we'd have a nice, refreshing meal.

480
00:18:57,517 --> 00:18:59,117
What could it be?

481
00:18:59,717 --> 00:19:01,687
What is it?

482
00:19:01,857 --> 00:19:03,617
- Gosh, the water is very shallow.
- I know.

483
00:19:04,227 --> 00:19:05,547
You said that you'd give us food.

484
00:19:05,957 --> 00:19:07,256
I thought...

485
00:19:07,257 --> 00:19:08,396
- Aren't you hungry?
- Yes.

486
00:19:08,397 --> 00:19:09,427
Yes, ma'am.

487
00:19:09,497 --> 00:19:11,636
You'll be heading off to eat.

488
00:19:11,637 --> 00:19:13,296
- Yes?
- Before you head there,

489
00:19:13,297 --> 00:19:14,836
you're going to change clothes...

490
00:19:14,837 --> 00:19:16,566
because of the dress code.

491
00:19:16,567 --> 00:19:17,837
Seriously?

492
00:19:18,207 --> 00:19:19,276
What kind of restaurant makes you wear boots...

493
00:19:19,277 --> 00:19:20,737
just to go eat?

494
00:19:22,207 --> 00:19:24,477
(Today's restaurant is a little challenging.)

495
00:19:24,877 --> 00:19:26,576
(You're recommended to wear chest waders, )

496
00:19:26,577 --> 00:19:28,717
(the kind you normally wear for work.)

497
00:19:29,047 --> 00:19:30,816
(A belt-style life jacket as a bonus)

498
00:19:30,817 --> 00:19:31,817
(Tapping)

499
00:19:32,217 --> 00:19:33,656
(A lightning-fast change of clothes...)

500
00:19:33,657 --> 00:19:35,627
(is done.)

501
00:19:35,957 --> 00:19:38,127
- Are we taking the boat there?
- Yes.

502
00:19:38,927 --> 00:19:40,396
(The siblings' current location)

503
00:19:40,397 --> 00:19:43,567
(Is today's mysterious restaurant hiding somewhere out there?)

504
00:19:43,767 --> 00:19:44,767
Let's go.

505
00:19:44,768 --> 00:19:46,436
- Let's go.
- Let's go, captain.

506
00:19:46,437 --> 00:19:47,566
(Being dramatic)

507
00:19:47,567 --> 00:19:50,006
- Hello.
- Nice to meet you, sir.

508
00:19:50,007 --> 00:19:51,306
(Climbing aboard the boat headed to the restaurant)

509
00:19:51,307 --> 00:19:52,407
Thank you.

510
00:19:52,737 --> 00:19:54,076
We'll be back.

511
00:19:54,077 --> 00:19:57,447
(It feels like they're being dragged away.)

512
00:19:58,447 --> 00:20:02,047
("To the Restaurant," 2026)

513
00:20:02,647 --> 00:20:05,357
- Are you really giving us food?
- How will we eat there?

514
00:20:05,757 --> 00:20:06,786
I'm scared.

515
00:20:06,787 --> 00:20:08,227
(A suspicious voice)

516
00:20:08,687 --> 00:20:12,027
(It's a brutal struggle of those who simply want a meal.)

517
00:20:12,657 --> 00:20:13,826
We can't let our guard down.

518
00:20:13,827 --> 00:20:16,427
("We can never let our guard down" by the siblings)

519
00:20:16,727 --> 00:20:18,166
- We're here.
- Pardon?

520
00:20:18,167 --> 00:20:19,413
(Right then, the boat stops in the middle of the river.)

521
00:20:19,437 --> 00:20:22,107
- Please take off your life vest.
- You have to go into the water.

522
00:20:22,567 --> 00:20:23,636
You have to go into the water.

523
00:20:23,637 --> 00:20:25,007
(We have to go where?)

524
00:20:25,377 --> 00:20:26,377
(For our bewildered siblings...)

525
00:20:26,378 --> 00:20:27,406
(Seomjin River, Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment)

526
00:20:27,407 --> 00:20:30,247
(Here's some basic information.)

527
00:20:31,117 --> 00:20:32,716
(The Seomjin River is a brackish zone...)

528
00:20:32,717 --> 00:20:34,317
(where freshwater and seawater meet.)

529
00:20:34,947 --> 00:20:37,416
(The tide comes in and goes out at regular times, )

530
00:20:37,417 --> 00:20:39,887
(changing the water depth.)

531
00:20:40,417 --> 00:20:43,057
(And that means you can just walk right into the water.)

532
00:20:43,557 --> 00:20:46,037
(They have to go into the water to get to today's restaurant.)

533
00:20:46,857 --> 00:20:48,097
Should we get off the boat now?

534
00:20:48,367 --> 00:20:49,797
(Striding)

535
00:20:50,267 --> 00:20:51,766
(You Seok, who headed to the edge of the boat without hesitating...)

536
00:20:51,767 --> 00:20:53,637
He just jumped right off the boat.

537
00:20:54,867 --> 00:20:56,437
(He enters the water right away.)

538
00:20:56,937 --> 00:20:58,076
(Right after that, the next member enters the water.)

539
00:20:58,077 --> 00:20:59,737
Oh, gosh. This has never happened before.

540
00:21:01,107 --> 00:21:03,807
My goodness. This is so fascinating.

541
00:21:05,117 --> 00:21:07,077
(The water is so cool that it makes them scream.)

542
00:21:07,987 --> 00:21:09,463
- Put your legs in first.
- I'm scared.

543
00:21:09,487 --> 00:21:10,846
- It's fun, Jung Ah.
- Oh, gosh.

544
00:21:10,847 --> 00:21:12,216
(What kind of meal will they have?)

545
00:21:12,217 --> 00:21:13,386
She just can't keep her balance.

546
00:21:13,387 --> 00:21:14,763
(What kind of meal will they have?)

547
00:21:14,787 --> 00:21:17,797
- We shouldn't fall down.
- We can't all fall down together.

548
00:21:18,697 --> 00:21:19,757
(Laughing)

549
00:21:20,197 --> 00:21:22,697
This is such an exciting experience.

550
00:21:23,267 --> 00:21:24,696
But I don't see any food here.

551
00:21:24,697 --> 00:21:25,817
(Sun Young charges forward.)

552
00:21:26,067 --> 00:21:27,067
Please don't come too close.

553
00:21:27,067 --> 00:21:28,067
(Urgent hand gesture)

554
00:21:28,068 --> 00:21:31,037
Where is our food? You said you'd give us food.

555
00:21:31,707 --> 00:21:32,777
That's not food.

556
00:21:33,137 --> 00:21:34,707
Gosh. Did you lie to us?

557
00:21:35,207 --> 00:21:37,577
- You'll eat first.
- Okay. Good.

558
00:21:38,317 --> 00:21:40,577
- The water is cool, right?
- Yes, this is so nice.

559
00:21:41,017 --> 00:21:42,916
You're going to eat in the water.

560
00:21:42,917 --> 00:21:44,816
- Are we eating while standing here?
- We're eating in the water?

561
00:21:44,817 --> 00:21:45,887
(Pardon?)

562
00:21:46,187 --> 00:21:47,557
We're eating in the water?

563
00:21:48,357 --> 00:21:50,133
(You just said it was nice to be in the cool water.)

564
00:21:50,157 --> 00:21:52,197
Then give us some swim tubes so we can sit on them.

565
00:21:52,757 --> 00:21:55,696
Ever heard of a floating breakfast? Like what they do in Bali.

566
00:21:55,697 --> 00:21:57,127
- "Flirting?"
- What is that?

567
00:21:57,667 --> 00:21:59,467
"Flirting" means hitting on someone.

568
00:22:00,137 --> 00:22:01,366
- It's "floating."
- "Floating?"

569
00:22:01,367 --> 00:22:02,767
- As in to be buoyant?
- Yes.

570
00:22:03,207 --> 00:22:05,106
- It means the food is served...
- "Fly?"

571
00:22:05,107 --> 00:22:07,177
- right on the water.
- That sounds nice.

572
00:22:07,907 --> 00:22:11,247
(You enjoy a floating meal on the gently rippling water.)

573
00:22:11,817 --> 00:22:13,537
(It's popular at vacation spots like Bali.)

574
00:22:14,017 --> 00:22:16,746
(The trendy meal that everyone is talking about...)

575
00:22:16,747 --> 00:22:18,386
- Oh, yes!
- This is fun.

576
00:22:18,387 --> 00:22:20,386
- We can enjoy food in the water.
- This is awesome.

577
00:22:20,387 --> 00:22:22,227
- Thank you.
- It's the bibimbap from earlier.

578
00:22:22,987 --> 00:22:24,733
(Filmed with the permission of the local fishing community)

579
00:22:24,757 --> 00:22:26,273
(Making its way into the siblings' hearts)

580
00:22:26,297 --> 00:22:29,327
- This is what they do at a hotel.
- In the swimming pool.

581
00:22:29,427 --> 00:22:32,036
They serve food like this at a luxurious swimming pool.

582
00:22:32,037 --> 00:22:33,066
I really like things like this.

583
00:22:33,067 --> 00:22:35,807
- Is this marsh clam?
- It's marsh clam bibimbap. Nice.

584
00:22:37,307 --> 00:22:41,647
(They'll enjoy marsh clam bibimbap with 4 side dishes in the water.)

585
00:22:42,207 --> 00:22:44,817
(Bibimbap with a mountain of fresh marsh clams)

586
00:22:45,377 --> 00:22:48,817
(And four different side dishes to choose from!)

587
00:22:49,417 --> 00:22:51,887
(The local fishermen often enjoy their meals like this too!)

588
00:22:52,417 --> 00:22:54,977
(They refuel with home-packed food in the middle of their work.)

589
00:22:56,187 --> 00:22:58,357
(The cool water boosts their appetites.)

590
00:22:58,757 --> 00:23:00,596
- Nice. We have gochujang.
- What's under there?

591
00:23:00,597 --> 00:23:03,326
- Jung Ah, I said I craved bibimbap.
- Right. You said that.

592
00:23:03,327 --> 00:23:04,327
Dig in.

593
00:23:04,437 --> 00:23:07,097
- Thank you so much.
- It looks delicious.

594
00:23:07,597 --> 00:23:09,407
Thank you so much.

595
00:23:10,167 --> 00:23:11,236
Dig in.

596
00:23:11,237 --> 00:23:12,557
(Yoon Seo smells the food first.)

597
00:23:12,777 --> 00:23:14,406
- Seriously...
- It looks tasty.

598
00:23:14,407 --> 00:23:16,147
I'm so happy right now.

599
00:23:17,077 --> 00:23:20,347
(The fresh aroma of marsh clams stimulates their appetites.)

600
00:23:20,677 --> 00:23:21,987
(Drizzling)

601
00:23:22,217 --> 00:23:24,757
- This is nice.
- I can't believe this.

602
00:23:25,057 --> 00:23:26,856
I want more of it, You Seok.

603
00:23:26,857 --> 00:23:29,757
(Adding chogochujang that will make it spicy, sour, and sweet)

604
00:23:30,257 --> 00:23:31,726
This is so much fun.

605
00:23:31,727 --> 00:23:32,927
(It's time to mix everything.)

606
00:23:33,057 --> 00:23:34,097
Marsh clam bibimbap is...

607
00:23:35,097 --> 00:23:37,727
(Sun Young takes a big bite before everyone else.)

608
00:23:37,897 --> 00:23:38,936
(Nodding)

609
00:23:38,937 --> 00:23:40,067
- Is it good?
- Yes.

610
00:23:40,897 --> 00:23:42,167
(The frown of the truth)

611
00:23:42,737 --> 00:23:43,967
- It's good.
- It looks tasty.

612
00:23:45,107 --> 00:23:46,737
I've only heard of marsh clam soup.

613
00:23:47,537 --> 00:23:49,476
(Taking a bite)

614
00:23:49,477 --> 00:23:52,047
Let's leave it here and just hold our bowls.

615
00:23:53,217 --> 00:23:54,217
What's this?

616
00:23:55,347 --> 00:23:56,946
- It's good.
- It's so good.

617
00:23:56,947 --> 00:23:58,563
(It's so tasty that it makes Sun Young shudder with joy.)

618
00:23:58,587 --> 00:24:01,287
(The perfect harmony of savory marsh clams and spicy chogochujang)

619
00:24:02,257 --> 00:24:03,326
It's so good.

620
00:24:03,327 --> 00:24:05,033
(Vegetables kick the umami up another notch.)

621
00:24:05,057 --> 00:24:07,597
(Jung Ah takes a big bite too.)

622
00:24:08,367 --> 00:24:10,797
(The bibimbap gets her stamp of approval.)

623
00:24:11,737 --> 00:24:14,367
(She tries marsh clam jeon, one of the side dishes.)

624
00:24:16,807 --> 00:24:17,876
It's perfect.

625
00:24:17,877 --> 00:24:19,197
(This is what I'm talking about.)

626
00:24:19,477 --> 00:24:21,206
They have their meals while standing here?

627
00:24:21,207 --> 00:24:22,783
(You Seok has a spoonful of bibimbap first.)

628
00:24:22,807 --> 00:24:24,977
(And then, he picks up a sausage.)

629
00:24:25,347 --> 00:24:27,217
(It goes straight into his mouth.)

630
00:24:27,987 --> 00:24:30,147
(And he nods in satisfaction.)

631
00:24:31,787 --> 00:24:33,716
This is such a rare experience.

632
00:24:33,717 --> 00:24:35,333
(The side dishes are perfect matches for the bibimbap.)

633
00:24:35,357 --> 00:24:36,426
Kimchi.

634
00:24:36,427 --> 00:24:38,727
(They pick up some kimchi.)

635
00:24:39,697 --> 00:24:42,143
(And the two sisters put the kimchi in their mouths at the same time.)

636
00:24:42,167 --> 00:24:43,167
This is refreshing.

637
00:24:43,927 --> 00:24:46,597
It's so nice to have a meal here. I really like this.

638
00:24:47,237 --> 00:24:50,007
Thank you so much for this delicious meal.

639
00:24:50,737 --> 00:24:51,737
Thank you, tvN.

640
00:24:52,807 --> 00:24:54,053
(They're too busy eating to care.)

641
00:24:54,077 --> 00:24:55,597
- Cheer up and work hard.
- Of course.

642
00:24:57,447 --> 00:25:00,677
(She continues enjoying her food energetically.)

643
00:25:01,717 --> 00:25:02,717
It's so good.

644
00:25:03,787 --> 00:25:06,217
(She takes another bite without a break.)

645
00:25:07,257 --> 00:25:11,227
(The siblings fell in love with marsh clam bibimbap.)

646
00:25:11,857 --> 00:25:15,297
(The flavor and texture of marsh clams make them devour the food.)

647
00:25:16,197 --> 00:25:18,667
(While everyone else is enjoying their food...)

648
00:25:18,897 --> 00:25:20,067
(Clattering)

649
00:25:21,537 --> 00:25:22,617
Put your bowl on your head.

650
00:25:22,667 --> 00:25:24,737
If your bowl is not empty, you can't go home today.

651
00:25:25,507 --> 00:25:26,536
Empty your bowl and put it on your head.

652
00:25:26,537 --> 00:25:28,407
(The leftover exterminator is here.)

653
00:25:28,977 --> 00:25:31,547
- Shall we get to work now?
- Wait, Sun Young.

654
00:25:31,777 --> 00:25:34,076
- Wait. We're still eating.
- Wait.

655
00:25:34,077 --> 00:25:35,546
- You Seok and I are still...
- Hurry up and come.

656
00:25:35,547 --> 00:25:37,493
- We're still eating!
- We should get to work now.

657
00:25:37,517 --> 00:25:38,857
(Returning)

658
00:25:38,987 --> 00:25:41,286
- I'll hold this. Go ahead and eat.
- Let me just have one more bite.

659
00:25:41,287 --> 00:25:42,786
(The younger members can't stop eating.)

660
00:25:42,787 --> 00:25:44,027
You might get an upset stomach.

661
00:25:44,357 --> 00:25:47,757
(The last bite)

662
00:25:48,067 --> 00:25:49,127
That was a good meal.

663
00:25:51,397 --> 00:25:55,167
(It was a perfect meal.)

664
00:25:55,437 --> 00:25:56,477
- We...
- Shall we go now?

665
00:25:57,467 --> 00:26:00,477
- prepared a gift for you.
- Here you go. Where should it go?

666
00:26:00,807 --> 00:26:02,577
(They give the staff a gift in return.)

667
00:26:02,907 --> 00:26:05,427
- Here you go. We really enjoyed it.
- Here's our gift for you.

668
00:26:05,747 --> 00:26:07,076
(Bye, floating meal. It was fun.)

669
00:26:07,077 --> 00:26:08,086
This is so fun.

670
00:26:08,087 --> 00:26:09,486
- Bye.
- Catch.

671
00:26:09,487 --> 00:26:11,147
(Bye, floating meal. It was fun.)

672
00:26:11,287 --> 00:26:12,357
Let's get to work now.

673
00:26:12,717 --> 00:26:14,487
- Did you enjoy your meal?
- Yes.

674
00:26:15,527 --> 00:26:18,656
We're here at the mouth of the Seomjin River,

675
00:26:18,657 --> 00:26:20,526
which flows between Hadong in South Gyeongsang Province...

676
00:26:20,527 --> 00:26:22,087
and Gwangyang in South Jeolla Province.

677
00:26:22,327 --> 00:26:24,367
What you're going to harvest here are...

678
00:26:24,797 --> 00:26:27,767
marsh clams, the treasure of the Seomjin River...

679
00:26:28,037 --> 00:26:30,567
where 80 percent of Korea's supply is produced.

680
00:26:32,107 --> 00:26:34,437
(The mountain of marsh clams from earlier...)

681
00:26:35,147 --> 00:26:38,247
The Seomjin River marsh clams taste clean and refreshing.

682
00:26:39,217 --> 00:26:41,046
We harvest them from early April...

683
00:26:41,047 --> 00:26:43,187
to the end of October.

684
00:26:43,617 --> 00:26:46,387
(Marsh clams, the small treasure of the Seomjin River)

685
00:26:47,257 --> 00:26:49,556
The river offers a great environment for marsh clams.

686
00:26:49,557 --> 00:26:51,473
(The Seomjin River, the nation's top marsh clam producer...)

687
00:26:51,497 --> 00:26:53,377
First of all, the water quality is exceptional.

688
00:26:53,927 --> 00:26:55,896
The water is completely free of any pollution sources.

689
00:26:55,897 --> 00:26:58,043
(It boasts pristine water quality, creating the perfect habitat.)

690
00:26:58,067 --> 00:26:59,107
The bottom of the river...

691
00:26:59,667 --> 00:27:02,106
is covered in fine sand.

692
00:27:02,107 --> 00:27:04,143
(These clean freshwater clams are hidden in the fine sand.)

693
00:27:04,167 --> 00:27:06,806
That's why the marsh clams here are tasty and produced in high volume.

694
00:27:06,807 --> 00:27:09,547
(Hadong's first seasonal food: Marsh clams)

695
00:27:10,277 --> 00:27:11,516
Marsh clams are in season now.

696
00:27:11,517 --> 00:27:14,687
Yes. So, you're going to harvest marsh clams.

697
00:27:14,987 --> 00:27:17,186
As for your pay,

698
00:27:17,187 --> 00:27:20,117
we'll give you ten dollars per kilogram of marsh clams.

699
00:27:20,717 --> 00:27:22,397
- But a marsh clam is this tiny.
- Got it.

700
00:27:22,787 --> 00:27:24,326
- Got it. Okay.
- Let's meet...

701
00:27:24,327 --> 00:27:26,797
the boat captain who will teach you...

702
00:27:27,097 --> 00:27:29,367
- A boat captain?
- how the work is done.

703
00:27:30,367 --> 00:27:32,237
- Hello.
- Hello.

704
00:27:32,937 --> 00:27:34,136
Did you all eat a lot?

705
00:27:34,137 --> 00:27:35,736
- Yes, we really enjoyed the food.
- Yes, we really enjoyed the food.

706
00:27:35,737 --> 00:27:38,107
- This must look strange to you.
- Yes.

707
00:27:38,977 --> 00:27:41,136
- Ta-da.
- Oh, you rake them in with this.

708
00:27:41,137 --> 00:27:43,006
(Today's harvesting tool looks unfamiliar.)

709
00:27:43,007 --> 00:27:45,817
I'm going to give you a quick guide...

710
00:27:45,917 --> 00:27:47,977
on how to harvest marsh clams.

711
00:27:48,447 --> 00:27:51,857
We're here at the Seomjin River, famous for its marsh clams.

712
00:27:52,587 --> 00:27:54,956
The harvesting method you'll use today is...

713
00:27:54,957 --> 00:27:57,477
called Sonteul Fishery System, as in "hand net fishing method."

714
00:27:57,557 --> 00:27:58,557
Hand shaking method?

715
00:27:58,897 --> 00:27:59,957
(Laughing)

716
00:28:00,427 --> 00:28:02,196
It's a marsh clam harvesting method...

717
00:28:02,197 --> 00:28:03,797
done completely by hand with a tool,

718
00:28:04,267 --> 00:28:06,337
instead of using machines.

719
00:28:06,697 --> 00:28:09,336
The method is listed...

720
00:28:09,337 --> 00:28:11,457
as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System.

721
00:28:12,277 --> 00:28:13,797
(The Seomjin River marsh clam harvest)

722
00:28:14,437 --> 00:28:17,246
This method has been passed down since the prehistoric era.

723
00:28:17,247 --> 00:28:18,247
(They still practice the method from the prehistoric era.)

724
00:28:18,248 --> 00:28:21,046
Continuing the tradition practiced by our ancestors,

725
00:28:21,047 --> 00:28:23,417
we use this clamming rake called "georaengi."

726
00:28:23,587 --> 00:28:24,957
It weighs over 10kg.

727
00:28:26,057 --> 00:28:29,056
This method, done purely by hand with a simple tool,

728
00:28:29,057 --> 00:28:31,577
is listed as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System.

729
00:28:31,827 --> 00:28:33,757
(Scrape the sand with a georaengi.)

730
00:28:34,627 --> 00:28:36,597
(Next, shake the sand out of the georaengi.)

731
00:28:38,237 --> 00:28:41,567
(Then move the marsh clams and gravel into the basin.)

732
00:28:44,007 --> 00:28:47,007
Working with a georaengi is very exhausting even for pros.

733
00:28:49,047 --> 00:28:51,516
It's because you have to drag it backward against the current.

734
00:28:51,517 --> 00:28:53,723
(It's physically taxing as you have to move against the current.)

735
00:28:53,747 --> 00:28:55,746
It may seem easy to drag the georaengi,

736
00:28:55,747 --> 00:28:57,816
but it actually requires enormous leg strength.

737
00:28:57,817 --> 00:28:59,497
(The legs feel like lead under the water.)

738
00:29:00,517 --> 00:29:01,756
Even though it's such hard work...

739
00:29:01,757 --> 00:29:03,833
(Nevertheless, they stick to the traditional method.)

740
00:29:03,857 --> 00:29:05,656
We take great pride in what we do.

741
00:29:05,657 --> 00:29:08,403
(The Seomjin River fishermen are proud of this traditional heritage.)

742
00:29:08,427 --> 00:29:10,797
(Siblings)

743
00:29:11,067 --> 00:29:13,567
Ta-da. This is a georaengi.

744
00:29:13,667 --> 00:29:15,107
Got it. Georaengi.

745
00:29:15,207 --> 00:29:16,367
Here's how you use it.

746
00:29:16,907 --> 00:29:20,377
Push the georaengi 2cm to 3 cm deep into the sand.

747
00:29:20,477 --> 00:29:22,407
- Yes.
- Dig gently into the sand.

748
00:29:23,447 --> 00:29:25,817
(The georaengi digs into the sand.)

749
00:29:26,047 --> 00:29:28,787
Rest the pole against your right shoulder, right on the seam.

750
00:29:29,247 --> 00:29:31,887
And keep your hands wide apart on the handle.

751
00:29:32,187 --> 00:29:34,717
Then lean back slightly and step backward.

752
00:29:35,327 --> 00:29:36,533
As you drag it along, you can feel something going into it, right?

753
00:29:36,557 --> 00:29:37,626
- The sand goes in.
- The sand.

754
00:29:37,627 --> 00:29:39,056
Marsh clams go in...

755
00:29:39,057 --> 00:29:40,497
- along with the sand.
- Yes.

756
00:29:42,427 --> 00:29:47,067
(A sandstorm swirls right into the georaengi.)

757
00:29:47,637 --> 00:29:49,837
Once the georaengi feels heavy enough,

758
00:29:50,207 --> 00:29:51,877
tilt it forward slightly.

759
00:29:52,307 --> 00:29:53,876
- The sand goes in, right?
- Yes.

760
00:29:53,877 --> 00:29:55,597
Then shake the georaengi from side to side.

761
00:29:56,007 --> 00:29:57,947
(Shaking)

762
00:29:58,417 --> 00:30:00,657
The sand will wash out, leaving only marsh clams behind.

763
00:30:01,147 --> 00:30:02,217
You can feel it, right?

764
00:30:02,647 --> 00:30:04,586
- I felt it.
- Marsh clams went in, right?

765
00:30:04,587 --> 00:30:06,287
- Yes.
- There's a lot of them.

766
00:30:06,587 --> 00:30:08,093
(Marsh clams and gravel are left in the georaengi.)

767
00:30:08,117 --> 00:30:11,186
After shaking it out, lift the georaengi up...

768
00:30:11,187 --> 00:30:12,226
and pour your harvest in the basin.

769
00:30:12,227 --> 00:30:14,197
(Pour everything in the plastic basin.)

770
00:30:14,257 --> 00:30:15,326
So I should hold it there.

771
00:30:15,327 --> 00:30:16,327
Yes, hold it like this.

772
00:30:16,427 --> 00:30:17,497
Here. Try it.

773
00:30:17,897 --> 00:30:19,936
It needs to dig 2cm to 3cm deep into the sand.

774
00:30:19,937 --> 00:30:21,297
You have incredible arm muscles.

775
00:30:21,637 --> 00:30:23,237
That's why I catch a lot of marsh clams.

776
00:30:23,437 --> 00:30:25,037
You can do it. Try to pull it.

777
00:30:25,907 --> 00:30:27,306
Let's all do our best.

778
00:30:27,307 --> 00:30:28,876
- Got it. Thank you.
- Yes. Thank you.

779
00:30:28,877 --> 00:30:30,307
(The marsh clam harvest begins.)

780
00:30:32,517 --> 00:30:33,576
Against the current.

781
00:30:33,577 --> 00:30:36,057
(They're working in the river for the first time on the show.)

782
00:30:37,647 --> 00:30:40,457
(It's all new to Jung Ah, but she takes her cautious step.)

783
00:30:42,927 --> 00:30:46,627
(Shaking)

784
00:30:47,327 --> 00:30:49,567
(After removing the sand and lifting up the georaengi, )

785
00:30:49,967 --> 00:30:51,927
(she just has to pour everything into the basin.)

786
00:30:52,997 --> 00:30:53,997
(Sighing)

787
00:30:53,998 --> 00:30:55,006
(A loud rattle)

788
00:30:55,007 --> 00:30:56,866
Oh, no. My goodness.

789
00:30:56,867 --> 00:30:58,737
(Flustered)

790
00:30:59,807 --> 00:31:00,807
Oh, gosh.

791
00:31:00,808 --> 00:31:02,546
(The georaengi is quite heavy, making it hard to handle.)

792
00:31:02,547 --> 00:31:03,607
Oh, my goodness.

793
00:31:05,577 --> 00:31:09,017
(Yoon Seo slowly pulls her georaengi against the current.)

794
00:31:10,447 --> 00:31:13,457
(After a massive swirl of sandstorm...)

795
00:31:14,687 --> 00:31:17,857
Left, right. Again.

796
00:31:18,997 --> 00:31:20,256
(She lifts up the georaengi to check the inside.)

797
00:31:20,257 --> 00:31:21,597
(Impressed)

798
00:31:22,127 --> 00:31:23,766
(Groaning)

799
00:31:23,767 --> 00:31:25,927
(As expected, she struggles with the heavy georaengi.)

800
00:31:26,397 --> 00:31:27,796
(Struggling)

801
00:31:27,797 --> 00:31:28,807
Oh, my goodness.

802
00:31:29,167 --> 00:31:30,367
Don't go.

803
00:31:31,037 --> 00:31:32,907
(On top of that, the basin floats away.)

804
00:31:33,677 --> 00:31:35,847
(Grunting)

805
00:31:36,577 --> 00:31:38,147
(She barely manages her first harvest.)

806
00:31:38,877 --> 00:31:41,617
(She's pouring every last marsh clam into the basin.)

807
00:31:42,817 --> 00:31:44,847
(You Seok moves fast against the current.)

808
00:31:45,757 --> 00:31:47,557
(Striding)

809
00:31:48,787 --> 00:31:51,257
(The sand rushes in at an incredible speed.)

810
00:31:52,157 --> 00:31:54,227
(He shakes the sand out vigorously.)

811
00:31:55,397 --> 00:31:57,196
(Gasping and exclaiming)

812
00:31:57,197 --> 00:31:59,677
(You Seok harvested a noticeably large amount of marsh clams.)

813
00:32:00,467 --> 00:32:03,337
(He lifts up the georaengi easily.)

814
00:32:04,337 --> 00:32:06,907
(Pouring)

815
00:32:08,207 --> 00:32:11,347
(Quite a mountain of marsh clams piled up in one go.)

816
00:32:12,317 --> 00:32:14,617
(Carefully moving)

817
00:32:14,947 --> 00:32:17,547
I didn't know marsh clams lived in the sand.

818
00:32:19,617 --> 00:32:22,987
(Sun Young gives off the vibe of a marsh clam master.)

819
00:32:24,527 --> 00:32:26,596
(Empty)

820
00:32:26,597 --> 00:32:28,327
(But her harvest is surprisingly small.)

821
00:32:29,527 --> 00:32:31,667
Sun Young, I've been working the same row as you.

822
00:32:31,867 --> 00:32:32,867
Sorry?

823
00:32:33,137 --> 00:32:34,737
There must not be much left there.

824
00:32:35,537 --> 00:32:36,807
(Realizing)

825
00:32:39,437 --> 00:32:41,797
(She was working right where Yoon Seo had already cleared.)

826
00:32:42,447 --> 00:32:43,506
(No wonder there were no marsh clams.)

827
00:32:43,507 --> 00:32:44,577
Okay. Hold on.

828
00:32:44,907 --> 00:32:46,707
I'm afraid there must not be much left there.

829
00:32:46,747 --> 00:32:48,487
We should divide up the areas.

830
00:32:49,387 --> 00:32:51,587
You Seok, our working areas don't overlap, right?

831
00:32:51,717 --> 00:32:52,817
I'll avoid your row.

832
00:33:01,297 --> 00:33:04,197
(Heading upstream along the Seomjin River, )

833
00:33:06,037 --> 00:33:10,207
(everyone is staying focused while pulling the georaengi.)

834
00:33:13,737 --> 00:33:15,137
I should put my weight on the back.

835
00:33:15,447 --> 00:33:16,907
(Focused)

836
00:33:18,077 --> 00:33:21,517
(It seems peaceful above water, )

837
00:33:22,447 --> 00:33:24,687
(but there's a massive sandstorm underneath.)

838
00:33:25,317 --> 00:33:26,586
(Shaking)

839
00:33:26,587 --> 00:33:27,917
Oh, my. You Seok is amazing.

840
00:33:28,987 --> 00:33:29,987
Way to go.

841
00:33:31,757 --> 00:33:34,597
(One scoop at a time, he slowly fills the basin.)

842
00:33:35,167 --> 00:33:36,526
(Staring)

843
00:33:36,527 --> 00:33:38,897
All those are marsh clams. I can see them.

844
00:33:40,167 --> 00:33:44,837
(Peering into the water, you can see the marsh clams.)

845
00:33:45,377 --> 00:33:47,006
(Moving the georaengi easily)

846
00:33:47,007 --> 00:33:48,846
(Struggling)

847
00:33:48,847 --> 00:33:50,947
(Breathing heavily)

848
00:33:52,317 --> 00:33:55,387
(She's tussling with the georaengi.)

849
00:33:58,457 --> 00:33:59,916
I can't lose a single clam.

850
00:33:59,917 --> 00:34:01,257
(Holding her breath)

851
00:34:02,157 --> 00:34:04,286
(Every single marsh clam is precious.)

852
00:34:04,287 --> 00:34:05,357
Okay.

853
00:34:12,397 --> 00:34:15,866
(Yoon Seo lifts up the georaengi with all her might.)

854
00:34:15,867 --> 00:34:17,607
(Staggering)

855
00:34:18,167 --> 00:34:20,807
Gosh, it's so heavy. Wait. Hold on.

856
00:34:21,307 --> 00:34:23,346
Wait. Stay still.

857
00:34:23,347 --> 00:34:24,747
(Dancing with the georaengi)

858
00:34:25,777 --> 00:34:27,057
(She practically threw it down.)

859
00:34:27,617 --> 00:34:28,947
This is the hardest part.

860
00:34:29,617 --> 00:34:31,517
(Sweating)

861
00:34:32,587 --> 00:34:35,527
(Sun Young, too, is lifting up the georaengi with all her might.)

862
00:34:36,387 --> 00:34:37,757
(Sighing and grunting)

863
00:34:38,327 --> 00:34:40,127
My gosh. It's so hard to lift it up.

864
00:34:44,567 --> 00:34:45,766
(A georaengi weighs about 15kg.)

865
00:34:45,767 --> 00:34:48,007
(The weight of the georaengi is an unexpected variable.)

866
00:34:48,097 --> 00:34:49,137
Okay.

867
00:34:52,407 --> 00:34:55,207
This may look easy, but it's actually really hard.

868
00:34:56,107 --> 00:34:57,547
It takes massive arm strength.

869
00:34:59,247 --> 00:35:01,176
(Groaning)

870
00:35:01,177 --> 00:35:02,287
I'm not tired.

871
00:35:02,647 --> 00:35:03,917
(Brainwashing herself)

872
00:35:04,387 --> 00:35:07,716
Aren't your feet hurting? There's so much pressure on them.

873
00:35:07,717 --> 00:35:09,657
- My toes are hurting.
- Right?

874
00:35:10,327 --> 00:35:11,556
The boots are...

875
00:35:11,557 --> 00:35:13,597
(The boots are painfully squeezing their feet.)

876
00:35:14,457 --> 00:35:16,467
(The pain remains completely hidden above water.)

877
00:35:17,097 --> 00:35:19,337
Should we harvest about 18kg?

878
00:35:20,397 --> 00:35:21,467
No, 20kg.

879
00:35:21,867 --> 00:35:22,937
That's 200 dollars.

880
00:35:23,737 --> 00:35:25,736
We're only getting ten dollars per kilogram.

881
00:35:25,737 --> 00:35:26,807
It's so tough.

882
00:35:28,207 --> 00:35:29,446
(Their faces say it all.)

883
00:35:29,447 --> 00:35:31,167
(They think harvesting 20kg is impossible.)

884
00:35:33,377 --> 00:35:34,547
I'll harvest 10kg.

885
00:35:34,917 --> 00:35:36,417
(You Seok shows strong confidence.)

886
00:35:36,887 --> 00:35:37,946
No, I can do it.

887
00:35:37,947 --> 00:35:39,307
(It boosts Yoon Seo's confidence.)

888
00:35:39,487 --> 00:35:40,617
Let's do this.

889
00:35:40,817 --> 00:35:41,987
Let's do this.

890
00:35:42,587 --> 00:35:44,627
(Let's go for it! Our goal is 20kg of marsh clams!)

891
00:35:44,727 --> 00:35:46,997
I just wish I could lift this easily.

892
00:35:47,697 --> 00:35:49,897
(She puts on gloves and gets back to work.)

893
00:35:51,327 --> 00:35:52,797
(Here comes the georaengi.)

894
00:35:59,307 --> 00:36:01,877
(Groaning)

895
00:36:04,077 --> 00:36:05,246
(Yoon Seo pushes forward...)

896
00:36:05,247 --> 00:36:06,393
(with that distinct art-major determination.)

897
00:36:06,417 --> 00:36:08,747
It's so heavy. It's no joke.

898
00:36:10,347 --> 00:36:13,957
(Diving into the water)

899
00:36:17,557 --> 00:36:19,327
(Jung Ah seems to be having trouble.)

900
00:36:20,097 --> 00:36:21,896
(She lifts the georaengi.)

901
00:36:21,897 --> 00:36:22,897
What is this?

902
00:36:22,898 --> 00:36:24,197
It's making it way too heavy.

903
00:36:27,167 --> 00:36:28,837
Jung Ah, did you perhaps catch a fish?

904
00:36:29,437 --> 00:36:30,707
It's not a fish.

905
00:36:31,267 --> 00:36:33,077
It's a strange thing.

906
00:36:33,477 --> 00:36:35,577
It's so heavy. Gosh, it's heavy.

907
00:36:36,747 --> 00:36:38,546
Gosh. What is this?

908
00:36:38,547 --> 00:36:39,547
(The strange thing)

909
00:36:40,147 --> 00:36:41,917
(Throwing it back into the water)

910
00:36:44,647 --> 00:36:45,786
(A shout of satisfaction)

911
00:36:45,787 --> 00:36:46,987
That's what I'm talking about.

912
00:36:47,387 --> 00:36:48,417
This is good.

913
00:36:50,027 --> 00:36:53,097
(She puts the georaengi on the basin in one go.)

914
00:36:53,897 --> 00:36:55,026
(Falling)

915
00:36:55,027 --> 00:36:56,896
Oh, no. They're slipping away... What a waste.

916
00:36:56,897 --> 00:36:59,697
(Harvesting them with her own hands makes every single clam precious.)

917
00:36:59,997 --> 00:37:01,137
I can't lose a single clam.

918
00:37:01,267 --> 00:37:04,007
(Patting the georaengi to praise its hard work)

919
00:37:04,907 --> 00:37:06,007
Gosh, I'm tired.

920
00:37:06,437 --> 00:37:08,037
From now on, whenever I eat marsh clams,

921
00:37:08,477 --> 00:37:09,877
I will never waste a single one.

922
00:37:10,577 --> 00:37:13,317
(Sun Young's basin drifts away, heading to Yoon Seo.)

923
00:37:13,817 --> 00:37:15,217
Sun Young, you harvested a lot.

924
00:37:15,847 --> 00:37:16,967
How did you harvest so many?

925
00:37:18,087 --> 00:37:21,257
(Sun Young harvested quite a lot of marsh clams.)

926
00:37:22,757 --> 00:37:24,587
There's a downside to this work.

927
00:37:25,357 --> 00:37:27,196
I can't sit down.

928
00:37:27,197 --> 00:37:28,597
(Disappointed)

929
00:37:29,797 --> 00:37:31,097
(Standing still)

930
00:37:31,467 --> 00:37:32,937
Can't we just do this on our knees?

931
00:37:33,737 --> 00:37:34,766
(Yes, you can.)

932
00:37:34,767 --> 00:37:36,007
(She's kneeling in the water.)

933
00:37:37,167 --> 00:37:39,777
(She tries it right away.)

934
00:37:41,637 --> 00:37:43,847
Sun Young, are you working on your knees?

935
00:37:44,347 --> 00:37:45,417
No.

936
00:37:46,017 --> 00:37:47,457
Why is the water so deep over there?

937
00:37:47,847 --> 00:37:49,117
Why is it so deep over there?

938
00:37:51,017 --> 00:37:53,187
It's too deep. It's dangerous.

939
00:37:54,187 --> 00:37:55,857
I see a lot of marsh clams here, Jung Ah.

940
00:37:56,857 --> 00:37:59,197
What? Why is it only so deep there?

941
00:38:00,457 --> 00:38:01,756
- Why?
- Sun Young, step backward...

942
00:38:01,757 --> 00:38:03,027
in that position.

943
00:38:03,267 --> 00:38:04,727
(That's a bit hard.)

944
00:38:05,667 --> 00:38:06,737
Step backward.

945
00:38:06,837 --> 00:38:08,907
(Moving backward)

946
00:38:09,207 --> 00:38:11,407
Come over here. It's too deep there.

947
00:38:11,667 --> 00:38:13,807
No. I'm good at swimming.

948
00:38:14,007 --> 00:38:15,107
(Laughing silently)

949
00:38:15,807 --> 00:38:17,006
Hey.

950
00:38:17,007 --> 00:38:18,687
What if you suddenly fall into deep water?

951
00:38:19,277 --> 00:38:20,346
I'll be fine.

952
00:38:20,347 --> 00:38:23,187
I would sacrifice my life for this show.

953
00:38:24,447 --> 00:38:26,717
(Laughing hard)

954
00:38:27,217 --> 00:38:28,617
Yoon Seo, kneel down like I do.

955
00:38:28,957 --> 00:38:31,327
- Kneel down?
- Pretend you're deep in the water.

956
00:38:31,457 --> 00:38:32,703
Pretend you're deep in the water like me.

957
00:38:32,727 --> 00:38:35,667
Jung Ah, it's so deep here.

958
00:38:36,027 --> 00:38:37,726
Get out of there. Why are you staying there?

959
00:38:37,727 --> 00:38:39,266
- Get out of there.
- It's so deep here.

960
00:38:39,267 --> 00:38:40,737
(Acting her heart out)

961
00:38:40,937 --> 00:38:42,366
- What are you talking about?
- It's so deep here.

962
00:38:42,367 --> 00:38:43,837
It's this deep here.

963
00:38:44,537 --> 00:38:45,937
(Staring)

964
00:38:46,677 --> 00:38:48,407
(You Seok goes to rescue Yoon Seo.)

965
00:38:48,977 --> 00:38:50,047
Oh, my.

966
00:38:50,407 --> 00:38:51,877
It gets deeper from there. Look.

967
00:38:52,047 --> 00:38:53,216
- It gets deeper.
- We're not joking.

968
00:38:53,217 --> 00:38:54,753
(You Seok is getting closer to Yoon Seo.)

969
00:38:54,777 --> 00:38:56,886
It's getting deeper, right?

970
00:38:56,887 --> 00:38:58,446
(Sinking deeper with every step)

971
00:38:58,447 --> 00:38:59,617
It's deep.

972
00:39:00,657 --> 00:39:01,756
Why is it so deep here?

973
00:39:01,757 --> 00:39:02,856
(The master of slapstick comedy)

974
00:39:02,857 --> 00:39:04,227
It's too deep here, Jung Ah.

975
00:39:04,827 --> 00:39:05,997
What are you guys doing?

976
00:39:06,227 --> 00:39:07,497
(Embarrassed)

977
00:39:07,927 --> 00:39:08,927
It's so deep.

978
00:39:08,928 --> 00:39:10,403
(The oldest is completely pranked, and the others are loving it.)

979
00:39:10,427 --> 00:39:11,967
Just hurry up already!

980
00:39:13,537 --> 00:39:14,897
(Shouting out of embarrassment)

981
00:39:15,337 --> 00:39:17,057
Jung Ah, I'll go back to the shallow water.

982
00:39:17,607 --> 00:39:19,077
Seriously, it's so deep here.

983
00:39:19,237 --> 00:39:21,406
Maybe I should rest it against my head.

984
00:39:21,407 --> 00:39:22,707
(The encore performance)

985
00:39:23,777 --> 00:39:25,977
(Happy)

986
00:39:26,817 --> 00:39:28,417
I think I can do this on my knees.

987
00:39:29,987 --> 00:39:31,016
Watch me.

988
00:39:31,017 --> 00:39:33,587
(Sun Young is actually trying to do it on her knees.)

989
00:39:34,557 --> 00:39:36,526
(Checking)

990
00:39:36,527 --> 00:39:37,527
(Surprised)

991
00:39:37,528 --> 00:39:39,127
Oh, I can actually do this on my knees.

992
00:39:39,627 --> 00:39:40,756
(Fascinated)

993
00:39:40,757 --> 00:39:42,597
I got this big of a harvest, even on my knees.

994
00:39:42,797 --> 00:39:43,797
Look.

995
00:39:43,798 --> 00:39:45,343
(She harvested more marsh clams than expected.)

996
00:39:45,367 --> 00:39:47,967
Didn't I get just as much as when I was standing?

997
00:39:48,967 --> 00:39:50,037
Right?

998
00:39:50,767 --> 00:39:51,837
One, two.

999
00:39:52,777 --> 00:39:54,507
(Chanting)

1000
00:39:55,707 --> 00:39:58,547
(The heavier the georaengi gets, the more marsh clams pile up.)

1001
00:40:02,987 --> 00:40:05,417
(She's working so hard with bulging veins.)

1002
00:40:05,657 --> 00:40:07,057
This is good exercise. It's nice.

1003
00:40:07,887 --> 00:40:09,026
It burns my biceps.

1004
00:40:09,027 --> 00:40:10,227
(Chuckling)

1005
00:40:11,127 --> 00:40:13,997
(They're working out with sand at the Seomjin River Gym.)

1006
00:40:14,557 --> 00:40:17,667
Who would ever go marsh clam digging in Seomjin River?

1007
00:40:17,897 --> 00:40:19,797
- Only we can do this.
- This is fun.

1008
00:40:22,307 --> 00:40:23,966
(They've harvested marsh clams...)

1009
00:40:23,967 --> 00:40:28,007
(for 1 hour.)

1010
00:40:32,917 --> 00:40:35,717
(Doing her best)

1011
00:40:37,017 --> 00:40:39,587
(A heap of marsh clams Sun Young harvested)

1012
00:40:41,557 --> 00:40:42,857
(Sighing)

1013
00:40:43,187 --> 00:40:46,127
This is tough. Isn't it so hard to hoist them up?

1014
00:40:46,757 --> 00:40:47,997
I think...

1015
00:40:48,867 --> 00:40:50,867
- we're going to be sore tomorrow.
- Really?

1016
00:40:50,997 --> 00:40:53,197
We're working against the current.

1017
00:40:54,037 --> 00:40:55,267
On top of it, it's very heavy.

1018
00:40:56,437 --> 00:40:57,507
So it's really tough.

1019
00:41:00,477 --> 00:41:02,107
It's so beautiful.

1020
00:41:03,447 --> 00:41:05,076
This place is magnificent.

1021
00:41:05,077 --> 00:41:06,397
(Totally immersed in the scenery)

1022
00:41:06,577 --> 00:41:07,816
Look at Jung Ah.

1023
00:41:07,817 --> 00:41:10,847
(Sun Young catches her breath while enjoying the scenery.)

1024
00:41:11,747 --> 00:41:14,117
Sun Young is sitting there slacking off, Jung Ah.

1025
00:41:14,987 --> 00:41:16,057
Sun Young.

1026
00:41:16,757 --> 00:41:18,227
(Pretending not to hear it)

1027
00:41:18,557 --> 00:41:20,797
(Laughing)

1028
00:41:21,357 --> 00:41:24,367
- Yes, Jung Ah. What is it?
- You've been sitting there a while.

1029
00:41:26,097 --> 00:41:28,167
I'm just saying because it bothers me.

1030
00:41:28,907 --> 00:41:30,166
(Laughing)

1031
00:41:30,167 --> 00:41:32,237
"It's nothing. It just bothers me."

1032
00:41:33,437 --> 00:41:34,507
I should get up now.

1033
00:41:34,977 --> 00:41:36,547
I was about to get up.

1034
00:41:37,607 --> 00:41:38,977
(Go, Sun Young.)

1035
00:41:40,447 --> 00:41:42,327
(She resumes clamming after the stern warning.)

1036
00:41:43,347 --> 00:41:45,817
(Yes. I, Sun Young, )

1037
00:41:45,887 --> 00:41:46,916
Hwagae Market

1038
00:41:46,917 --> 00:41:49,057
(will focus solely on work today.)

1039
00:41:49,857 --> 00:41:53,257
(She is working so hard it's leaving marks on her arms.)

1040
00:41:54,157 --> 00:41:56,397
(At that moment, the cameraman tries to shoot You Seok.)

1041
00:41:57,327 --> 00:41:58,427
(Bumping)

1042
00:41:58,797 --> 00:42:01,597
Come on. Someone's trying to work here.

1043
00:42:02,097 --> 00:42:03,107
Seriously.

1044
00:42:03,907 --> 00:42:06,137
I can't work like this.

1045
00:42:06,707 --> 00:42:07,736
(Laughing)

1046
00:42:07,737 --> 00:42:08,836
(Retreating)

1047
00:42:08,837 --> 00:42:10,637
(He retreats quickly before things get ugly.)

1048
00:42:11,647 --> 00:42:14,477
(In the meantime, she compares hers with You Seok's.)

1049
00:42:15,277 --> 00:42:17,417
Why is the difference in our harvest so huge?

1050
00:42:18,147 --> 00:42:19,386
(You Seok, Sun Young)

1051
00:42:19,387 --> 00:42:21,333
(You Seok has way more marsh clams than Sun Young.)

1052
00:42:21,357 --> 00:42:23,926
- I really did my best, though.
- I really worked hard.

1053
00:42:23,927 --> 00:42:26,496
- I worked hard too.
- I didn't even say a word.

1054
00:42:26,497 --> 00:42:29,127
I've been working without saying a word.

1055
00:42:29,867 --> 00:42:32,097
But why the huge difference?

1056
00:42:33,497 --> 00:42:36,907
Running across Jeolla Province and Gyeongsang Province

1057
00:42:37,767 --> 00:42:39,407
I'm going to catch up to You Seok.

1058
00:42:41,207 --> 00:42:43,447
(Georaengi is on.)

1059
00:42:46,747 --> 00:42:48,277
Our viewers like hard workers.

1060
00:42:53,887 --> 00:42:56,027
Gosh. Look at these.

1061
00:42:56,427 --> 00:42:57,527
- Look.
- That's a lot.

1062
00:42:59,257 --> 00:43:00,257
Good job.

1063
00:43:01,327 --> 00:43:02,867
(Proud)

1064
00:43:03,627 --> 00:43:04,627
She's unstoppable.

1065
00:43:06,337 --> 00:43:08,867
I'm going to rake up everything. Just you wait.

1066
00:43:10,567 --> 00:43:11,636
Go.

1067
00:43:11,637 --> 00:43:13,637
(The unstoppable train is on.)

1068
00:43:14,507 --> 00:43:15,627
Are you doing okay, Jung Ah?

1069
00:43:17,047 --> 00:43:18,107
I'm okay.

1070
00:43:19,147 --> 00:43:20,217
- Are you?
- Gosh.

1071
00:43:23,247 --> 00:43:25,657
I feel like I'm one with the clamming rake.

1072
00:43:25,987 --> 00:43:28,417
- You look so relaxed.
- I'm relaxed.

1073
00:43:29,127 --> 00:43:30,156
- I mean...
- He's good, though.

1074
00:43:30,157 --> 00:43:31,227
- Really?
- Yes.

1075
00:43:31,727 --> 00:43:33,297
I think this kind of work suits me.

1076
00:43:33,757 --> 00:43:34,827
I might quit acting.

1077
00:43:35,827 --> 00:43:36,996
(You Seok is so good at it...)

1078
00:43:36,997 --> 00:43:38,543
(that he even thinks about switching careers.)

1079
00:43:38,567 --> 00:43:40,087
How far are you going to go, You Seok?

1080
00:43:43,237 --> 00:43:47,807
(He is raking up all the marsh clams in Seomjin River.)

1081
00:43:50,577 --> 00:43:52,177
How many kilograms do you think this is?

1082
00:43:52,877 --> 00:43:53,977
(Staring)

1083
00:43:54,217 --> 00:43:55,247
About 500g?

1084
00:43:56,547 --> 00:43:57,587
That's annoying.

1085
00:43:57,647 --> 00:43:59,287
They don't really weigh much.

1086
00:43:59,987 --> 00:44:03,057
- Let me see yours. You have a lot.
- I think I have about 2kg.

1087
00:44:04,527 --> 00:44:05,897
Did you say a full bucket is 40kg?

1088
00:44:06,727 --> 00:44:08,573
(It weighs about 40kg when the bucket is full.)

1089
00:44:08,597 --> 00:44:10,727
Then we need to fill a quarter of it to reach 10kg.

1090
00:44:11,697 --> 00:44:13,967
- Is this only 1.5kg or 1kg?
- Yes.

1091
00:44:14,767 --> 00:44:15,767
Sir.

1092
00:44:15,967 --> 00:44:17,006
(Asking the captain for confirmation out of desperation)

1093
00:44:17,007 --> 00:44:19,437
Sir, how much will this weigh?

1094
00:44:19,977 --> 00:44:21,807
- Let me see.
- About 2kg?

1095
00:44:22,407 --> 00:44:23,647
About 2kg?

1096
00:44:25,077 --> 00:44:26,077
You've harvested a lot.

1097
00:44:26,777 --> 00:44:28,257
- This will be about 4kg.
- Goodness.

1098
00:44:29,117 --> 00:44:30,717
- Just a little...
- But just now...

1099
00:44:30,847 --> 00:44:32,857
- About 20m up there is better.
- Here?

1100
00:44:33,687 --> 00:44:34,786
(She's fired up as it's more than she expected.)

1101
00:44:34,787 --> 00:44:36,227
(Dancing)

1102
00:44:36,787 --> 00:44:38,057
(Doing her best)

1103
00:44:38,257 --> 00:44:40,526
- Have you harvested a lot?
- Pardon?

1104
00:44:40,527 --> 00:44:41,596
Gosh. You got a lot.

1105
00:44:41,597 --> 00:44:43,126
- Is this a lot?
- Yes.

1106
00:44:43,127 --> 00:44:44,667
How many kilograms will it weigh?

1107
00:44:45,997 --> 00:44:47,066
It's over 1kg, right?

1108
00:44:47,067 --> 00:44:49,307
It's about 6 to 7kg, not just 1kg.

1109
00:44:50,667 --> 00:44:52,677
- What is he saying?
- It's about 6kg.

1110
00:44:53,677 --> 00:44:55,137
Jung Ah, we made so much money.

1111
00:44:55,607 --> 00:44:58,406
Let's say that's 5kg, then we've made 50 dollars.

1112
00:44:58,407 --> 00:44:59,946
Altogether, it'll be about 200 dollars.

1113
00:44:59,947 --> 00:45:01,587
(Maybe they'll reach their 20-kg target.)

1114
00:45:01,677 --> 00:45:03,787
Gosh. We'll have to eat meat again.

1115
00:45:04,547 --> 00:45:07,186
Let's go there. Let's head that way now.

1116
00:45:07,187 --> 00:45:08,433
(A final push to harvest more marsh clams)

1117
00:45:08,457 --> 00:45:09,787
Let's hurry. You Seok, come on.

1118
00:45:11,527 --> 00:45:12,557
Let's go.

1119
00:45:12,957 --> 00:45:14,027
Let's go.

1120
00:45:14,997 --> 00:45:15,997
Let's go.

1121
00:45:15,998 --> 00:45:18,467
(Let's hit our 20-kg mark!)

1122
00:45:23,307 --> 00:45:24,627
I won't lose a single marsh clam.

1123
00:45:25,337 --> 00:45:26,877
(Her hand's booster mode is on.)

1124
00:45:27,737 --> 00:45:28,906
One, two, three.

1125
00:45:28,907 --> 00:45:31,077
Hey, look. It's a lot, isn't it?

1126
00:45:32,407 --> 00:45:33,477
It's way more than before.

1127
00:45:36,147 --> 00:45:37,787
Hello, everyone. Can you see it?

1128
00:45:38,317 --> 00:45:39,717
(Proud)

1129
00:45:41,787 --> 00:45:44,087
After making money in such a hard way,

1130
00:45:45,027 --> 00:45:46,147
I won't be able to spend it.

1131
00:45:47,857 --> 00:45:49,396
(He gets to understand the value of hard work...)

1132
00:45:49,397 --> 00:45:50,967
(after experiencing it himself.)

1133
00:45:52,937 --> 00:45:55,166
I'll show you how much I've changed.

1134
00:45:55,167 --> 00:45:56,613
(The hand-wrapped fishing was new to them as it was their first time.)

1135
00:45:56,637 --> 00:45:58,037
Yes. I'm good. That's right.

1136
00:45:58,237 --> 00:46:02,507
(But the siblings have gotten used to it quickly.)

1137
00:46:03,407 --> 00:46:04,927
Let me go to the deeper water quickly.

1138
00:46:05,477 --> 00:46:07,747
(They found a new kind of hand-wrapped fishing too.)

1139
00:46:08,317 --> 00:46:09,347
Gosh. I got a lot.

1140
00:46:11,347 --> 00:46:12,386
I got a lot.

1141
00:46:12,387 --> 00:46:14,707
(They have huge piles of marsh clams before they know it.)

1142
00:46:17,357 --> 00:46:18,687
Gosh. The breeze feels nice.

1143
00:46:19,397 --> 00:46:21,556
- Keep blowing.
- I want to dive in.

1144
00:46:21,557 --> 00:46:22,666
(They've been harvesting marsh clams for 2 hours.)

1145
00:46:22,667 --> 00:46:24,067
- Into the water.
- I know. Me too.

1146
00:46:24,667 --> 00:46:27,197
- Hey, Sun Young. Come back now.
- Yes.

1147
00:46:27,497 --> 00:46:28,897
Good job. You can come back now.

1148
00:46:29,407 --> 00:46:31,607
- No.
- She doesn't want to.

1149
00:46:32,067 --> 00:46:33,807
Let's start sorting now.

1150
00:46:34,207 --> 00:46:35,537
- Gosh. It's exciting.
- Okay.

1151
00:46:36,347 --> 00:46:38,277
Gosh. Okay. I got it.

1152
00:46:38,807 --> 00:46:39,816
Gosh. The water is deep here.

1153
00:46:39,817 --> 00:46:40,916
(The tide is coming in already.)

1154
00:46:40,917 --> 00:46:41,946
It's gotten deeper again.

1155
00:46:41,947 --> 00:46:43,223
(The tide is coming in already.)

1156
00:46:43,247 --> 00:46:44,316
Goodness.

1157
00:46:44,317 --> 00:46:45,917
(It's time to sort the harvested clams.)

1158
00:46:46,087 --> 00:46:47,587
Here's the last push.

1159
00:46:48,087 --> 00:46:49,116
(Hunting marsh clams until the very last minute)

1160
00:46:49,117 --> 00:46:50,187
I want more.

1161
00:46:51,287 --> 00:46:52,996
(Shaking off the last piece of the clams)

1162
00:46:52,997 --> 00:46:54,397
(Sun Young's proud of her.)

1163
00:46:54,857 --> 00:46:56,297
(Let's go.)

1164
00:46:56,797 --> 00:46:57,797
It was hard, wasn't it?

1165
00:46:58,127 --> 00:47:00,097
It was hard to lift them.

1166
00:47:01,037 --> 00:47:02,367
(Sighing)

1167
00:47:03,967 --> 00:47:06,007
(She gave it her all.)

1168
00:47:06,437 --> 00:47:07,736
Gosh. Yoon Seo.

1169
00:47:07,737 --> 00:47:09,246
(How much did Yoon Seo harvest without missing a single clam?)

1170
00:47:09,247 --> 00:47:10,747
It's about 4kg.

1171
00:47:12,677 --> 00:47:15,247
(Is it still 4kg?)

1172
00:47:15,517 --> 00:47:16,586
It's 4kg.

1173
00:47:16,587 --> 00:47:19,787
Gosh. Sun Young, you got a lot. It'll be about 6 to 7kg.

1174
00:47:20,317 --> 00:47:21,586
(Grunting)

1175
00:47:21,587 --> 00:47:22,857
Don't throw them away.

1176
00:47:23,157 --> 00:47:24,517
- Are there any clams left?
- Yes.

1177
00:47:25,387 --> 00:47:26,456
(She won't forgive him if he misses any clams.)

1178
00:47:26,457 --> 00:47:27,527
You're strong.

1179
00:47:28,127 --> 00:47:30,397
Jung Ah, you've harvested lots of stones.

1180
00:47:30,597 --> 00:47:33,067
You've got lots of stones and sand.

1181
00:47:33,297 --> 00:47:34,596
(Laughing)

1182
00:47:34,597 --> 00:47:36,906
- Lastly, the one to watch.
- Our star.

1183
00:47:36,907 --> 00:47:38,137
(How much did You Seok catch?)

1184
00:47:38,907 --> 00:47:40,736
Gosh. This will weigh about 10kg.

1185
00:47:40,737 --> 00:47:42,036
Yes. I've made 100 dollars.

1186
00:47:42,037 --> 00:47:44,647
Let's gather them in one place and start sorting.

1187
00:47:45,147 --> 00:47:46,216
Gosh.

1188
00:47:46,217 --> 00:47:47,737
(Pouring each harvest into one bucket)

1189
00:47:48,247 --> 00:47:49,247
Gosh. It's a lot.

1190
00:47:49,248 --> 00:47:50,317
(Heaped)

1191
00:47:51,147 --> 00:47:52,587
It's a lot when you put it together.

1192
00:47:53,017 --> 00:47:54,587
- I know.
- Yes.

1193
00:47:54,687 --> 00:47:56,856
We'll see how much we'll have after the sorting process.

1194
00:47:56,857 --> 00:47:58,256
Yes. We have to sort them thoroughly.

1195
00:47:58,257 --> 00:48:01,096
- We'll start sorting now.
- Okay.

1196
00:48:01,097 --> 00:48:03,526
Just like this, we can't boil them...

1197
00:48:03,527 --> 00:48:05,566
- or cook them.
- You're right.

1198
00:48:05,567 --> 00:48:07,607
- We need to sort stones, sand, - And empty shells.

1199
00:48:07,897 --> 00:48:09,907
- and empty shells.
- Yes.

1200
00:48:10,967 --> 00:48:13,977
- Do we have to do it by hand?
- One by one?

1201
00:48:14,077 --> 00:48:15,946
Exactly. Do we pick them out one by one?

1202
00:48:15,947 --> 00:48:17,623
(Don't tell me we'll pick out each of them.)

1203
00:48:17,647 --> 00:48:19,177
Goodness. Is that a sieve?

1204
00:48:19,747 --> 00:48:21,276
There's a sieve over there.

1205
00:48:21,277 --> 00:48:22,277
(The sieve is here.)

1206
00:48:22,278 --> 00:48:24,317
- Okay. Watch how it's done.
- Okay.

1207
00:48:25,287 --> 00:48:26,917
- You'll get a scoop of it.
- Okay.

1208
00:48:27,687 --> 00:48:28,856
(Pour them on the sieve.)

1209
00:48:28,857 --> 00:48:30,827
The current is flowing that way.

1210
00:48:32,027 --> 00:48:35,127
Spread them evenly. Then move gently.

1211
00:48:36,227 --> 00:48:37,427
Gently.

1212
00:48:37,697 --> 00:48:39,797
You're sending the empty shells forward.

1213
00:48:40,497 --> 00:48:42,496
Throw away the empty shells.

1214
00:48:42,497 --> 00:48:43,766
(The empty shells sweep forward.)

1215
00:48:43,767 --> 00:48:45,467
I see. Only the empty shells flow away.

1216
00:48:45,637 --> 00:48:48,177
- Right? The empty shells are gone.
- Yes.

1217
00:48:48,277 --> 00:48:50,677
The empty shells float forward because they're light, right?

1218
00:48:51,047 --> 00:48:53,576
They are pushed forward because the rocks are heavy.

1219
00:48:53,577 --> 00:48:54,646
(The heavy rocks roll backward.)

1220
00:48:54,647 --> 00:48:56,287
- Okay.
- There are so many empty shells.

1221
00:48:56,947 --> 00:48:59,228
- We might lose the clams.
- There are many empty shells.

1222
00:48:59,287 --> 00:49:01,786
If you do it wrong, you'll lose the clams, right?

1223
00:49:01,787 --> 00:49:02,857
What do we do, then?

1224
00:49:03,057 --> 00:49:05,257
Then you don't get paid today.

1225
00:49:05,687 --> 00:49:07,827
It may look easy, but it won't be when you try it.

1226
00:49:08,127 --> 00:49:10,847
We have to get rid of the rocks after pushing away the empty shells.

1227
00:49:11,167 --> 00:49:12,197
Look closely.

1228
00:49:12,997 --> 00:49:15,037
(Focused)

1229
00:49:15,797 --> 00:49:17,906
(Shaking)

1230
00:49:17,907 --> 00:49:19,407
- Good. They're gone.
- What?

1231
00:49:20,777 --> 00:49:24,007
(Only the rocks fall back.)

1232
00:49:24,577 --> 00:49:25,907
- Good. They're gone.
- What?

1233
00:49:26,317 --> 00:49:28,216
Come on. We can't do that.

1234
00:49:28,217 --> 00:49:30,087
We'll lose all our clams.

1235
00:49:30,417 --> 00:49:31,917
I'll just sort them by hand.

1236
00:49:32,317 --> 00:49:33,786
Then you won't be able to go home.

1237
00:49:33,787 --> 00:49:36,326
Okay. Let's get rid of the rocks.

1238
00:49:36,327 --> 00:49:38,757
When you move gently, only the rocks stay behind.

1239
00:49:39,357 --> 00:49:41,357
- See?
- Gosh.

1240
00:49:41,457 --> 00:49:42,526
And you toss them.

1241
00:49:42,527 --> 00:49:43,767
(Tossing only the rocks gently)

1242
00:49:43,827 --> 00:49:45,627
And you toss them. Okay?

1243
00:49:46,397 --> 00:49:47,466
(Flustered)

1244
00:49:47,467 --> 00:49:49,767
- That's ridiculous.
- How are we supposed to do that?

1245
00:49:50,137 --> 00:49:51,606
(How are we supposed to do that?)

1246
00:49:51,607 --> 00:49:54,077
- Okay. There's another way.
- That was like...

1247
00:49:54,277 --> 00:49:56,558
Okay. The rocks are at the back. Then you turn the sieve.

1248
00:49:57,347 --> 00:49:58,777
You toss them like this. Okay?

1249
00:49:59,517 --> 00:50:01,346
You just threw away five marsh clams.

1250
00:50:01,347 --> 00:50:02,746
Exactly. You shouldn't do it like that.

1251
00:50:02,747 --> 00:50:04,546
You lost a lot of our clams.

1252
00:50:04,547 --> 00:50:06,157
I shouldn't do the work for you.

1253
00:50:06,717 --> 00:50:09,787
Okay. You'll pour a scoop of them and try it yourselves.

1254
00:50:10,487 --> 00:50:11,726
Go, Jung Ah.

1255
00:50:11,727 --> 00:50:12,756
(Do we start sorting right away just like this?)

1256
00:50:12,757 --> 00:50:14,257
Okay. You'll get a sieve each.

1257
00:50:15,327 --> 00:50:17,567
Try to be as gentle as possible.

1258
00:50:18,827 --> 00:50:20,627
Why aren't the empty shells floating forward?

1259
00:50:21,637 --> 00:50:24,507
(Shaking back and forth)

1260
00:50:25,137 --> 00:50:26,237
They won't move.

1261
00:50:27,807 --> 00:50:30,747
(His marsh clams won't budge.)

1262
00:50:31,047 --> 00:50:32,276
They aren't moving forward.

1263
00:50:32,277 --> 00:50:33,317
(Clumsily)

1264
00:50:33,677 --> 00:50:34,847
How am I supposed to do it?

1265
00:50:37,917 --> 00:50:39,457
I think I have too many.

1266
00:50:40,317 --> 00:50:43,927
Okay. One, two. Gently. They are gone, right?

1267
00:50:44,657 --> 00:50:46,356
One, two, and toss.

1268
00:50:46,357 --> 00:50:48,496
I'm losing so many clams. Don't do it.

1269
00:50:48,497 --> 00:50:49,527
(Don't do it.)

1270
00:50:50,097 --> 00:50:51,227
(Protect our marsh clams.)

1271
00:50:51,627 --> 00:50:54,266
You're a spy to steal ours, right?

1272
00:50:54,267 --> 00:50:55,713
(He's even suspected of being a spy.)

1273
00:50:55,737 --> 00:50:57,237
(Focused)

1274
00:50:58,237 --> 00:50:59,607
I think I'm better at it now.

1275
00:51:01,407 --> 00:51:03,646
It's so interesting to see the rocks gathering at the back.

1276
00:51:03,647 --> 00:51:05,223
(Yoon Seo is the first one to get the hang of it.)

1277
00:51:05,247 --> 00:51:07,447
(Tossing only the empty shells away as she was told)

1278
00:51:08,517 --> 00:51:11,047
That's it. The empty shells got tossed now.

1279
00:51:11,387 --> 00:51:12,587
You're good.

1280
00:51:12,887 --> 00:51:15,216
- Dip it deeper. Gosh.
- Gosh.

1281
00:51:15,217 --> 00:51:17,227
You're losing all the clams.

1282
00:51:17,857 --> 00:51:18,987
It wasn't my fault.

1283
00:51:19,597 --> 00:51:21,097
You just touched it.

1284
00:51:21,227 --> 00:51:23,397
- Yoon Seo.
- You laid your hands on it.

1285
00:51:24,767 --> 00:51:27,736
Okay. As for the rocks, hold the sieve like this.

1286
00:51:27,737 --> 00:51:28,773
(Holding the sieve the other way around)

1287
00:51:28,797 --> 00:51:30,437
Try tossing them. Higher.

1288
00:51:30,567 --> 00:51:31,837
Higher. That's it.

1289
00:51:33,077 --> 00:51:35,437
(Replay: Only the rocks are tossed away.)

1290
00:51:36,547 --> 00:51:39,717
Turn it around, and pour the clams.

1291
00:51:41,617 --> 00:51:42,887
Pour them. Okay.

1292
00:51:44,287 --> 00:51:45,887
(Proud)

1293
00:51:46,987 --> 00:51:48,057
Okay.

1294
00:51:48,257 --> 00:51:49,927
Gosh. That's a lot.

1295
00:51:50,687 --> 00:51:52,087
Toss them. That's right.

1296
00:51:52,787 --> 00:51:54,126
Push it farther away.

1297
00:51:54,127 --> 00:51:55,226
(She executes what she has learned right away.)

1298
00:51:55,227 --> 00:51:57,227
That's it. The shells are tossed now.

1299
00:51:57,727 --> 00:52:00,407
(Is she going to toss away the rocks by herself while she's at it?)

1300
00:52:02,137 --> 00:52:03,567
The youngest one is the best at it.

1301
00:52:05,167 --> 00:52:07,677
She was an art major, so she's good with her hands.

1302
00:52:08,107 --> 00:52:09,746
(Bragging about her major for the millionth time)

1303
00:52:09,747 --> 00:52:11,577
Gosh. I'm glad I majored in art.

1304
00:52:12,547 --> 00:52:14,987
I think the whole country knows that I majored in art by now.

1305
00:52:15,247 --> 00:52:18,047
What did Yoon Seo excel at last time? Harvesting garlic?

1306
00:52:18,387 --> 00:52:20,857
- Yes.
- Yes. She was a garlic prodigy.

1307
00:52:20,987 --> 00:52:22,116
She was a prodigy on a garlic field.

1308
00:52:22,117 --> 00:52:24,357
(Next to a prodigy in the field of labor is...)

1309
00:52:24,757 --> 00:52:26,373
Why aren't the empty shells moving forward?

1310
00:52:26,397 --> 00:52:29,127
(the one who still hasn't gotten the hang of it.)

1311
00:52:31,027 --> 00:52:32,297
They won't go forward.

1312
00:52:34,037 --> 00:52:36,137
(No matter how hard he shakes it, they won't budge.)

1313
00:52:36,807 --> 00:52:38,566
(He was good at harvesting them.)

1314
00:52:38,567 --> 00:52:39,706
(Glancing)

1315
00:52:39,707 --> 00:52:41,237
- Why?
- Why can't I do it?

1316
00:52:42,337 --> 00:52:43,447
Move it like this.

1317
00:52:44,107 --> 00:52:46,447
No. Move the sieve like this underwater.

1318
00:52:46,647 --> 00:52:48,947
You push it down, then pull it toward you.

1319
00:52:49,717 --> 00:52:50,746
It's like rowing.

1320
00:52:50,747 --> 00:52:52,707
(He tries it calmly, following her instructions.)

1321
00:52:53,187 --> 00:52:54,187
Yes. They're floating.

1322
00:52:54,188 --> 00:52:56,017
(Finally, the empty shells are floating.)

1323
00:52:56,087 --> 00:52:57,187
You're doing great.

1324
00:52:58,457 --> 00:53:02,656
(After the tutoring, he's become faster at sorting.)

1325
00:53:02,657 --> 00:53:03,867
I think I'm done.

1326
00:53:05,367 --> 00:53:07,967
(You Seok sorts his first batch too.)

1327
00:53:08,737 --> 00:53:09,806
(At that moment)

1328
00:53:09,807 --> 00:53:10,867
(Staring)

1329
00:53:11,507 --> 00:53:12,837
I don't get it at all.

1330
00:53:14,007 --> 00:53:16,137
(She does what she was told to do.)

1331
00:53:16,607 --> 00:53:18,647
(But the rocks and the empty shells stay the same.)

1332
00:53:18,747 --> 00:53:21,077
(I can't go home until I sort them all.)

1333
00:53:21,517 --> 00:53:23,477
(There are so many clams that need to be sorted.)

1334
00:53:23,847 --> 00:53:25,287
Why don't we just...

1335
00:53:26,957 --> 00:53:29,187
(She starts picking them out by hand.)

1336
00:53:29,857 --> 00:53:32,427
(It's as fast as the traditional sorting method.)

1337
00:53:32,487 --> 00:53:34,727
I need to remove the rocks. There are so many.

1338
00:53:36,297 --> 00:53:39,467
(Picking)

1339
00:53:39,697 --> 00:53:41,136
(If it doesn't work, do what works.)

1340
00:53:41,137 --> 00:53:44,337
(And do it fast.)

1341
00:53:44,737 --> 00:53:45,966
There are so many rocks.

1342
00:53:45,967 --> 00:53:48,527
(Someone who is faster than anyone when it comes to manual work)

1343
00:53:51,877 --> 00:53:53,917
(This isn't fast-forwarded.)

1344
00:53:54,077 --> 00:53:58,117
These rocks are just like the chocolate named Dolmens.

1345
00:53:58,717 --> 00:53:59,717
It's the Stone Age.

1346
00:54:00,687 --> 00:54:01,687
(Immersed in finding the chocolate-shaped rocks)

1347
00:54:01,688 --> 00:54:03,487
Gosh. There are just so many rocks.

1348
00:54:04,687 --> 00:54:06,497
I feel like an otter now.

1349
00:54:07,097 --> 00:54:08,256
I'm picking the shells.

1350
00:54:08,257 --> 00:54:10,627
(She feels like an otter looking for clams.)

1351
00:54:12,127 --> 00:54:14,667
And the color of the rocks is similar to that of the clams.

1352
00:54:14,737 --> 00:54:17,867
Yes. I don't see them anymore.

1353
00:54:18,867 --> 00:54:20,277
They do look so much alike.

1354
00:54:21,407 --> 00:54:22,607
It's like Hidden Pictures.

1355
00:54:22,977 --> 00:54:24,647
They do look the same, Jung Ah.

1356
00:54:25,547 --> 00:54:28,347
Why do the rocks have a similar color?

1357
00:54:29,077 --> 00:54:31,847
The marsh clams have changed their colors to look like rocks...

1358
00:54:32,147 --> 00:54:33,457
so that they won't get caught.

1359
00:54:33,717 --> 00:54:35,956
They're like, "I'm a rock," just like a chameleon.

1360
00:54:35,957 --> 00:54:37,287
- Am I right, Jung Ah?
- I guess.

1361
00:54:37,757 --> 00:54:39,557
Do you think I won't be able to catch you?

1362
00:54:41,627 --> 00:54:43,897
(After sorting the marsh clams with a keen eye, )

1363
00:54:45,627 --> 00:54:47,136
(they deliver them to the rubber basin.)

1364
00:54:47,137 --> 00:54:48,537
When are we going to sort them all?

1365
00:54:49,937 --> 00:54:52,337
(Getting the refill of the clams right away)

1366
00:54:52,967 --> 00:54:54,007
You Seok, here.

1367
00:54:54,477 --> 00:54:55,606
That's good enough.

1368
00:54:55,607 --> 00:54:57,707
(Here come more marsh clams.)

1369
00:54:58,047 --> 00:55:00,917
(They've been sorting marsh clams for 1 hour.)

1370
00:55:01,717 --> 00:55:05,017
(After sorting the marsh clams with eagle eyes for a while, )

1371
00:55:05,747 --> 00:55:08,657
(their backs start to hurt.)

1372
00:55:09,287 --> 00:55:10,357
My feet hurt.

1373
00:55:11,227 --> 00:55:13,056
I can't lean against the boat because it's moving.

1374
00:55:13,057 --> 00:55:14,897
- It moves.
- It moves when I lean against it.

1375
00:55:15,527 --> 00:55:18,926
I had no idea harvesting marsh clams...

1376
00:55:18,927 --> 00:55:20,897
was this difficult and complicated.

1377
00:55:21,737 --> 00:55:23,437
Sorting is harder than harvesting.

1378
00:55:24,537 --> 00:55:26,706
Every single clam is precious when you harvested them yourselves.

1379
00:55:26,707 --> 00:55:27,806
- Yes.
- Yes.

1380
00:55:27,807 --> 00:55:30,007
When I had bibimbap,

1381
00:55:30,147 --> 00:55:32,106
I only had the clam meat.

1382
00:55:32,107 --> 00:55:34,547
I realized how precious that was.

1383
00:55:35,647 --> 00:55:38,147
I'll appreciate every ingredient from now on.

1384
00:55:39,417 --> 00:55:41,817
(With newfound appreciation, she scoops up another helping.)

1385
00:55:41,957 --> 00:55:43,087
(Drowning)

1386
00:55:43,357 --> 00:55:44,357
No!

1387
00:55:44,602 --> 00:55:45,742
(Drowning)

1388
00:55:46,002 --> 00:55:47,742
No! No.

1389
00:55:48,612 --> 00:55:50,012
(No!)

1390
00:55:50,542 --> 00:55:52,281
(While Yoon Seo cries out, )

1391
00:55:52,282 --> 00:55:53,282
No.

1392
00:55:53,283 --> 00:55:55,612
(their eyes are fixed only on the marsh clams.)

1393
00:55:56,112 --> 00:55:57,781
I didn't throw them away. I'm sorry.

1394
00:55:57,782 --> 00:56:00,222
- Okay. It's all right.
- It's okay to go underwater.

1395
00:56:00,322 --> 00:56:01,722
We don't care if you go underwater.

1396
00:56:02,222 --> 00:56:04,238
- But not the marsh clams.
- But don't let the clams drown.

1397
00:56:04,262 --> 00:56:05,462
Once you let them go,

1398
00:56:05,822 --> 00:56:08,132
you have to rake them up two more times.

1399
00:56:10,502 --> 00:56:12,262
Marsh clams are this precious, everyone.

1400
00:56:12,762 --> 00:56:14,378
They're more precious than your little sister.

1401
00:56:14,402 --> 00:56:15,431
(Sighing)

1402
00:56:15,432 --> 00:56:17,372
- She's upset.
- They're more precious.

1403
00:56:17,472 --> 00:56:19,418
Marsh clams that are more precious than Yoon Seo.

1404
00:56:19,442 --> 00:56:20,742
(He's the worst.)

1405
00:56:21,972 --> 00:56:23,458
- She's upset.
- Something was flying.

1406
00:56:23,482 --> 00:56:25,342
No. Water splashed.

1407
00:56:26,012 --> 00:56:28,773
Marsh Clams That Are More Precious Than Yoon Seo should be the brand.

1408
00:56:29,712 --> 00:56:30,952
(Laughing)

1409
00:56:31,322 --> 00:56:33,082
(Fuming with anger)

1410
00:56:33,692 --> 00:56:35,828
(You Seok grabs the marsh clams more precious than Yoon Seo.)

1411
00:56:35,852 --> 00:56:37,821
(Trudging)

1412
00:56:37,822 --> 00:56:39,362
(Kneeling out of the blue)

1413
00:56:39,722 --> 00:56:42,432
(Then I'll join him.)

1414
00:56:42,962 --> 00:56:44,362
The water keeps getting deeper.

1415
00:56:45,832 --> 00:56:46,972
I'll sit down too.

1416
00:56:47,302 --> 00:56:48,822
Gosh. My lower back feels much better.

1417
00:56:49,702 --> 00:56:50,872
Are you all on your knees?

1418
00:56:51,072 --> 00:56:53,002
- Yes.
- Yes. My back hurts a lot less.

1419
00:56:53,242 --> 00:56:54,542
Really? I'll try it too, then.

1420
00:56:56,142 --> 00:56:57,882
(Kneeling)

1421
00:56:58,282 --> 00:57:00,682
(Everyone is seated.)

1422
00:57:01,112 --> 00:57:04,082
(While everyone is working in the deep water, )

1423
00:57:04,852 --> 00:57:07,922
(someone looks really troubled.)

1424
00:57:09,992 --> 00:57:11,092
It's raining.

1425
00:57:11,592 --> 00:57:14,462
We have to work faster. It's sad to work in the rain.

1426
00:57:15,332 --> 00:57:16,932
I think I forgot how to speak.

1427
00:57:19,062 --> 00:57:21,432
Gosh. Doing nothing but work is making me depressed.

1428
00:57:25,402 --> 00:57:27,412
(What am I feeling?)

1429
00:57:27,572 --> 00:57:29,012
- Do a skit.
- I mean...

1430
00:57:30,482 --> 00:57:32,441
(A skit?)

1431
00:57:32,442 --> 00:57:33,982
The thing I always do?

1432
00:57:34,452 --> 00:57:35,782
The thing I do naturally?

1433
00:57:36,782 --> 00:57:38,452
Gosh. I'm dizzy.

1434
00:57:38,522 --> 00:57:41,292
We, who harvest marsh clams for a living, feel the same...

1435
00:57:41,722 --> 00:57:43,162
after harvesting them for two hours.

1436
00:57:43,562 --> 00:57:45,492
We get depressed and silent.

1437
00:57:46,322 --> 00:57:47,561
It's because we don't talk to one another...

1438
00:57:47,562 --> 00:57:49,208
- We become quiet.
- and only focus on one's own work.

1439
00:57:49,232 --> 00:57:50,902
You're only working...

1440
00:57:51,362 --> 00:57:53,572
because it's so hard. You don't even want to talk.

1441
00:57:54,572 --> 00:57:56,432
- That's exactly how I'm feeling.
- Right?

1442
00:57:56,502 --> 00:57:58,071
Sometimes,

1443
00:57:58,072 --> 00:58:00,512
I want to dump all the clams I harvested back into the river.

1444
00:58:01,672 --> 00:58:04,742
We feel exactly what you're feeling as we work, Sun Young.

1445
00:58:06,012 --> 00:58:08,452
Gosh. That's a strong story.

1446
00:58:13,622 --> 00:58:14,822
For goodness' sake.

1447
00:58:15,022 --> 00:58:16,192
Sun Young, your marsh clams!

1448
00:58:16,822 --> 00:58:17,921
(Drowning)

1449
00:58:17,922 --> 00:58:19,092
Gosh. The marsh clams.

1450
00:58:19,162 --> 00:58:20,192
I saved them.

1451
00:58:21,062 --> 00:58:22,062
(Fortunately, the marsh clams...)

1452
00:58:22,062 --> 00:58:23,062
(that are more precious than Sun Young are safe.)

1453
00:58:23,063 --> 00:58:25,132
- Are you all right, Sun Young - I'm fine.

1454
00:58:26,602 --> 00:58:28,102
- Goodness.
- Let's do this.

1455
00:58:28,402 --> 00:58:30,242
- Let's get it.
- Let's go.

1456
00:58:33,342 --> 00:58:34,642
(Sighing)

1457
00:58:36,642 --> 00:58:38,682
(Shaking them off)

1458
00:58:40,582 --> 00:58:42,412
(Getting a big scoop)

1459
00:58:42,612 --> 00:58:44,421
As for the sorting work you're doing,

1460
00:58:44,422 --> 00:58:47,352
our first-years find it the hardest.

1461
00:58:47,722 --> 00:58:49,522
- But it's our first day today.
- Exactly.

1462
00:58:50,062 --> 00:58:52,492
It's practically torture.

1463
00:58:53,492 --> 00:58:56,492
(But the siblings do it anyway.)

1464
00:58:56,702 --> 00:58:58,201
- Good.
- I keep picking the rocks out,

1465
00:58:58,202 --> 00:58:59,522
but more of them keep turning up.

1466
00:59:00,472 --> 00:59:03,332
I think more than half of these are rocks.

1467
00:59:04,542 --> 00:59:05,571
(When they gather the marsh clams they sort out by hand...)

1468
00:59:05,572 --> 00:59:06,842
We've done quite a bit.

1469
00:59:07,672 --> 00:59:08,841
There's a lot less now.

1470
00:59:08,842 --> 00:59:10,141
(They end up with this much.)

1471
00:59:10,142 --> 00:59:11,382
How many kilograms will it be?

1472
00:59:12,382 --> 00:59:14,422
Do you think we can make about 50 dollars, Jung Ah?

1473
00:59:14,852 --> 00:59:16,212
It's raining quite a bit.

1474
00:59:16,352 --> 00:59:19,552
Rain isn't the problem. The tide started coming in.

1475
00:59:19,622 --> 00:59:21,152
It'd be great if you could speed up.

1476
00:59:22,652 --> 00:59:23,892
We'll take it home as homework.

1477
00:59:24,892 --> 00:59:27,432
If you give us this as homework, we'll get it done by tomorrow.

1478
00:59:29,092 --> 00:59:31,031
Okay, let's check how much you have left.

1479
00:59:31,032 --> 00:59:33,232
- We're almost done.
- Are you?

1480
00:59:33,672 --> 00:59:34,802
I can see the bottom of it.

1481
00:59:36,072 --> 00:59:37,602
You could scoop the whole thing out.

1482
00:59:38,442 --> 00:59:39,502
- Me?
- Yes.

1483
00:59:40,972 --> 00:59:42,012
I'll pour it out for you.

1484
00:59:43,012 --> 00:59:44,712
Who got so much sand?

1485
00:59:45,082 --> 00:59:47,511
(He even checks the sorted marsh clams.)

1486
00:59:47,512 --> 00:59:50,251
Did you throw out all the clams while sorting?

1487
00:59:50,252 --> 00:59:52,052
The amount has reduced to half.

1488
00:59:52,252 --> 00:59:53,692
It won't even weigh 10kg.

1489
00:59:54,622 --> 00:59:56,521
Honestly, it's you who threw away...

1490
00:59:56,522 --> 00:59:57,692
about 1kg.

1491
00:59:58,692 --> 01:00:00,462
(Embarrassed)

1492
01:00:00,762 --> 01:00:01,992
Give us our pay for the 1kg.

1493
01:00:02,232 --> 01:00:03,762
Got it. I'll do that.

1494
01:00:03,962 --> 01:00:05,432
Look forward to it.

1495
01:00:05,502 --> 01:00:06,531
We're done.

1496
01:00:06,532 --> 01:00:08,932
(They're finally in the final stages of sorting.)

1497
01:00:09,302 --> 01:00:11,342
(Shaking off the very last batch)

1498
01:00:11,542 --> 01:00:14,212
You did well with the sorting. I don't see many stones.

1499
01:00:14,272 --> 01:00:15,272
- Right?
- Yes.

1500
01:00:15,342 --> 01:00:17,482
- We took our time to be thorough.
- We worked hard.

1501
01:00:18,012 --> 01:00:21,212
(They make sure to pick out every last stone.)

1502
01:00:21,682 --> 01:00:22,751
(Pouring the batch You Seok sorted)

1503
01:00:22,752 --> 01:00:23,782
Be careful.

1504
01:00:23,952 --> 01:00:24,982
To the very last clams.

1505
01:00:25,722 --> 01:00:26,951
Let's weigh it.

1506
01:00:26,952 --> 01:00:28,422
(It's finally time to weigh it.)

1507
01:00:28,622 --> 01:00:31,222
One scoop weighs about 4kg.

1508
01:00:31,722 --> 01:00:34,591
Okay. 1 scoop is 4kg.

1509
01:00:34,592 --> 01:00:36,902
(Today's rate is 10 dollars per kilo.)

1510
01:00:37,362 --> 01:00:38,631
(2 scoops)

1511
01:00:38,632 --> 01:00:39,632
Eighty dollars.

1512
01:00:41,202 --> 01:00:42,442
One hundred and twenty dollars.

1513
01:00:43,942 --> 01:00:45,142
One hundred and sixty dollars.

1514
01:00:45,842 --> 01:00:47,542
Am I wrong? Is something wrong?

1515
01:00:48,672 --> 01:00:50,511
- Okay, weigh it.
- Look how much is left.

1516
01:00:50,512 --> 01:00:51,612
Weigh it.

1517
01:00:52,082 --> 01:00:53,652
You toss them like this. Okay?

1518
01:00:54,082 --> 01:00:56,012
You just threw away five marsh clams.

1519
01:00:57,022 --> 01:01:01,322
(But we literally threw away so much.)

1520
01:01:01,852 --> 01:01:02,991
(Did they already reach 160 dollars?)

1521
01:01:02,992 --> 01:01:04,362
Okay, let's weigh it.

1522
01:01:05,092 --> 01:01:06,762
- Up it goes.
- What?

1523
01:01:06,862 --> 01:01:08,462
- Give it a second.
- It says 15kg.

1524
01:01:09,262 --> 01:01:10,502
Fifteen... Sorry?

1525
01:01:11,332 --> 01:01:13,102
(Seriously?)

1526
01:01:13,432 --> 01:01:15,701
Give it time for the water to drain out.

1527
01:01:15,702 --> 01:01:18,571
Earlier, to boost your expectations,

1528
01:01:18,572 --> 01:01:20,771
I said it wouldn't weigh much.

1529
01:01:20,772 --> 01:01:22,171
But it'll probably weigh about one mal.

1530
01:01:22,172 --> 01:01:23,812
- How heavy is that?
- It's 30kg.

1531
01:01:25,242 --> 01:01:27,212
We did it!

1532
01:01:27,452 --> 01:01:29,582
- Oh, no. Will you be okay?
- That's 300 dollars.

1533
01:01:29,852 --> 01:01:31,022
Will you be okay?

1534
01:01:31,122 --> 01:01:33,222
(Bagging up the rest of the marsh clams)

1535
01:01:33,352 --> 01:01:34,592
I see a few left.

1536
01:01:35,322 --> 01:01:37,821
Try to get as many to weigh. It could be 100g.

1537
01:01:37,822 --> 01:01:39,961
Digging for them shows you their worth, right?

1538
01:01:39,962 --> 01:01:41,292
- Totally.
- Yes.

1539
